PathwayData.put("data/reading_writing/units/L06U2.json",{"strand":"reading_writing","level":6,"cefr":"B1b","unit":2,"theme":"Our Changing Planet","essential_question":"How are humans changing the planet, and what can we do?","schema_version":"1.0","core_words":["climate","environment","recycle","renewable","solar","emissions","global","drought","flood","resource","reduce","reuse","carbon","sustainable"],"recycled_words":["environment","protect","pollution","plastic","conserve","fact","opinion","evidence","should","however","because","conclusion","persuade"],"rubrics":[{"id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","title":"Analytic writing rubric","traits":[{"name":"Ideas","name_ar":"الأفكار","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear, interesting and fully developed with strong details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة وممتعة ومدعومة بتفاصيل قوية."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear and have enough details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة ومعها تفاصيل كافية."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"My main idea is there but needs more details.","descriptor_ar":"فكرتي الرئيسية موجودة لكنها تحتاج إلى تفاصيل أكثر."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"My ideas are unclear or very few.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري غير واضحة أو قليلة جدًا."}]},{"name":"Organization","name_ar":"التنظيم","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Clear beginning, middle and end; ideas flow with linking words.","descriptor_ar":"بداية ووسط ونهاية واضحة، والأفكار مترابطة بكلمات الربط."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Clear order with some linking words.","descriptor_ar":"ترتيب واضح مع بعض كلمات الربط."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Some order, but parts are mixed up.","descriptor_ar":"يوجد بعض الترتيب لكن بعض الأجزاء غير مرتبة."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Ideas are not in order.","descriptor_ar":"الأفكار غير مرتبة."}]},{"name":"Vocabulary","name_ar":"المفردات","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"I use exact, varied words, including new unit words, correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات دقيقة ومتنوعة، منها كلمات الوحدة الجديدة، بشكل صحيح."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"I use some new words correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم بعض الكلمات الجديدة بشكل صحيح."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"I use simple words; some are repeated or wrong.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات بسيطة، وبعضها مكرر أو غير صحيح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"I use very few words; the meaning is hard to understand.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات قليلة جدًا ويصعب فهم المعنى."}]},{"name":"Sentence fluency","name_ar":"سلاسة الجمل","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My sentences are complete, varied in length and easy to read aloud.","descriptor_ar":"جملي كاملة ومتنوعة الطول وسهلة القراءة بصوت عالٍ."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Most sentences are complete, with some variety.","descriptor_ar":"معظم جملي كاملة مع بعض التنويع."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Many sentences are short or start the same way.","descriptor_ar":"كثير من جملي قصيرة أو تبدأ بالطريقة نفسها."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Sentences are incomplete or run together.","descriptor_ar":"جملي ناقصة أو متداخلة."}]},{"name":"Conventions","name_ar":"قواعد الكتابة","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Capitals, punctuation, spelling and grammar are almost always correct.","descriptor_ar":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم والإملاء والقواعد صحيحة دائمًا تقريبًا."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"A few mistakes that do not affect meaning.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء قليلة لا تؤثر في المعنى."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Several mistakes; the meaning is sometimes unclear.","descriptor_ar":"عدة أخطاء، والمعنى أحيانًا غير واضح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Many mistakes make it hard to read.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء كثيرة تجعل القراءة صعبة."}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS1","title":"Word Power: Our Changing Planet","lesson_type":"vocabulary","objectives":["read, say and use 14 words about our changing planet and 4 Tier 2 words (claim, relevant, valid, alternative)","use the prefixes re- (again / back), non- (not) and en- / em- (to make, to put into) to work out word meanings","build and spell new words with these prefixes and use them in context"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة ونطق واستخدام 14 كلمة عن كوكبنا المتغيّر و4 كلمات أكاديمية.","استخدام السوابق re- وnon- وen-/em- لاستنتاج معاني الكلمات.","بناء كلمات جديدة بهذه السوابق وتهجئتها واستخدامها في السياق."],"success_criteria":["I can use the environment words correctly in sentences.","I can explain the meaning of re-, non- and en- / em- with examples.","I can work out the meaning of a new word such as *nonrenewable* or *endanger* from its parts."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع استخدام كلمات البيئة في جمل بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع شرح معاني re- وnon- وen-/em- مع أمثلة.","أستطيع استنتاج معنى كلمة جديدة مثل nonrenewable أو endanger من أجزائها."],"map_area":"Vocabulary: Acquisition and Use","vocabulary":[{"word":"climate","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈklaɪmət/","definition":"the usual weather of a place over many years","arabic":"المناخ","example":"The climate of Taif is cooler than Riyadh's.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"environment","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪnˈvaɪrənmənt/","definition":"the air, water, land, plants and animals around us","arabic":"البيئة","example":"Plastic harms the environment.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"recycle","pos":"verb","ipa":"/riˈsaɪkəl/","definition":"to treat used things so they can be made into new things","arabic":"يعيد التدوير","example":"We recycle cans and paper at school.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"renewable","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/rɪˈnuəbəl/","definition":"able to be replaced naturally, like sunlight or wind","arabic":"متجدّد","example":"Solar power is a renewable energy.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"solar","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈsoʊlər/","definition":"using or coming from the sun","arabic":"شمسي","example":"Solar panels turn sunlight into electricity.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"emissions","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪˈmɪʃənz/","definition":"gases and smoke sent into the air, for example by cars and factories","arabic":"انبعاثات","example":"Cars produce emissions.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"global","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɡloʊbəl/","definition":"about the whole world","arabic":"عالمي","example":"Climate change is a global problem.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V07","tier":"core"},{"word":"drought","pos":"noun","ipa":"/draʊt/","definition":"a long time with little or no rain","arabic":"جفاف","example":"The drought dried up the wells.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V08","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V08","tier":"core"},{"word":"flood","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/flʌd/","definition":"a lot of water covering land that is usually dry","arabic":"فيضان / سيل","example":"Heavy rain caused a flood in the valley.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V09","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V09","tier":"core"},{"word":"resource","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrisɔrs/","definition":"something useful that people need, such as water, oil or land","arabic":"مورد","example":"Water is a precious resource.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V10","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V10","tier":"core"},{"word":"reduce","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈdus/","definition":"to make something smaller or less","arabic":"يقلّل","example":"Reduce the water you use.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V11","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V11","tier":"core"},{"word":"reuse","pos":"verb","ipa":"/riˈjuz/","definition":"to use something again","arabic":"يعيد الاستخدام","example":"Reuse your shopping bags.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V12","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V12","tier":"core"},{"word":"carbon","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkɑrbən/","definition":"a chemical element; the gas carbon dioxide made when fuel burns","arabic":"الكربون","example":"Trees take in carbon dioxide.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V13","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V13","tier":"core"},{"word":"sustainable","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/səˈsteɪnəbəl/","definition":"able to continue for a long time without harming the environment","arabic":"مستدام","example":"Saudi Arabia is building sustainable cities.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V14","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V14","tier":"core"},{"word":"claim","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/kleɪm/","definition":"a statement that something is true; to say that something is true","arabic":"ادعاء / يدّعي","example":"The writer's claim is that plastic bags should be banned.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V15","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V15","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"relevant","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈrɛləvənt/","definition":"closely connected to what you are talking about","arabic":"ذو صلة","example":"Give relevant evidence for your claim.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V16","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V16","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"valid","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈvælɪd/","definition":"based on good reasons or facts; acceptable","arabic":"سليم / صحيح / مقبول","example":"That is a valid point.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V17","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V17","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"alternative","pos":"noun / adjective","ipa":"/ɔlˈtɝnətɪv/","definition":"another choice you can use instead","arabic":"بديل","example":"Cloth bags are a good alternative to plastic bags.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V18","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V18","tier":"tier2"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **prefix** is a group of letters added to the **beginning** of a word to change its meaning. The base word keeps its spelling: *re + use = reuse*, *non + renewable = nonrenewable*.\n\n**re-** = **again** or **back**: *reuse* (use again), *recycle* (cycle back), *refill*, *rebuild*, *renewable* (able to be made new again). **non-** = **not**: *nonrenewable* (not renewable), *nonstop*, *nontoxic*, *nonliving*. **en- / em-** = **to make** or **to put into**: *endanger* (put in danger), *enable* (make able), *enlarge* (make larger), *enrich*. Use **em-** before **b** and **p**: *empower*, *embody*.\n\nTo work out a word: (1) **find the prefix**; (2) **find the base word**; (3) **join the meanings**; (4) **check** in the sentence. Careful: some words only *look* like they have a prefix (*read*, *never*, *enter*).","examples":["**re** + use → *reuse* = use again: *Reuse your water bottle.*","**re** + fill → *refill* = fill again: *Refill your bottle at the water station.*","**re** + new + able → *renewable* = can be made new again: *Wind is a renewable resource.*","**non** + renewable → *nonrenewable* = not renewable: *Oil is a nonrenewable resource.*","**non** + toxic → *nontoxic* = not poisonous: *Use nontoxic paint.*","**en** + danger → *endanger* = put in danger: *Plastic bags endanger sea turtles.*","**en** + able → *enable* = make able: *Solar panels enable homes to make their own electricity.*","**em** + power → *empower* = give power to: *Recycling clubs empower students to act.*","Not a prefix: *read*, *never* and *enter* – **re-**, **ne-** and **en-** here are not prefixes."],"common_errors":["✗ Oil is unrenewable. → ✓ Oil is nonrenewable. (Use **non-** with renewable.)","✗ We should use again our bags. → ✓ We should reuse our bags. (re- = again.)","✗ Pollution dangers the animals. → ✓ Pollution endangers the animals. (en- + danger = endanger.)","✗ enpower → ✓ empower (en- → **em-** before b and p.)","✗ recycle means to throw away. → ✓ recycle means to make used things into new things. (Check the meaning.)","✗ Reduce is the same as reuse. → ✓ Reduce = use less; reuse = use again. (Different words.)","✗ The environment are dirty. → ✓ The environment is dirty. (environment = singular.)"],"arabic":"السابقة مجموعة حروف تُضاف إلى بداية الكلمة لتغيير معناها، وتبقى الكلمة الأصل بتهجئتها: reuse وnonrenewable.\n\nre- تعني «مرة أخرى» أو «إلى الخلف» مثل reuse وrecycle وrefill، وnon- تعني «غير/ليس» مثل nonrenewable وnontoxic، وen-/em- تعني «يجعل» أو «يضع في» مثل endanger وenable وempower.\n\nتُكتب em- قبل الحرفين b وp. وانتبه: بعض الكلمات تبدو كأن لها سابقة وليست كذلك مثل read وenter.\n\nالخطوات: جِد السابقة، ثم الكلمة الأصل، ثم اجمع المعنيين، ثم تحقّق من الجملة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Our planet is changing – and the words we use to talk about it can change things too!","on_screen":"Our Changing Planet 🌍","visual":"Time-lapse of Earth from space; a desert, a sea and a green city."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Climate, environment, recycle, renewable, solar, emissions, global, drought, flood, resource, reduce, reuse, carbon, sustainable!","on_screen":"climate · environment · recycle · renewable · solar · emissions · global · drought · flood · resource · reduce · reuse · carbon · sustainable","visual":"Each word appears with an animated icon."},{"time":"01:30–02:05","narration":"Make a claim, give relevant and valid reasons, and suggest an alternative!","on_screen":"claim · relevant · valid · alternative","visual":"A debate stage with speech bubbles."},{"time":"02:05–03:30","narration":"Re- means again. Non- means not. En- means to make or to put into – and it becomes em- before b and p!","on_screen":"re- · non- · en- / em-","visual":"Prefix cards click onto base words: reuse, nonrenewable, endanger, empower."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Find the prefix, find the base, join the meanings – and check the sentence!","on_screen":"Prefix + base = meaning","visual":"A green checkmark appears over a sentence."}],"captions_ar":["كوكبنا يتغيّر، والكلمات التي نستخدمها للحديث عنه يمكنها أن تغيّر الأشياء أيضًا!","المناخ، البيئة، إعادة التدوير، متجدد، شمسي، انبعاثات، عالمي، جفاف، فيضان، مورد، يقلّل، يعيد الاستخدام، الكربون، مستدام!","قدّم ادعاءً، وأعطِ أسبابًا ذات صلة وسليمة، واقترح بديلًا!","re- تعني مرة أخرى، وnon- تعني ليس، وen- تعني يجعل أو يضع في، وتصبح em- قبل b وp!","جِد السابقة، وجِد الأصل، واجمع المعاني، وتحقق من الجملة!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, energetic female voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","instructions_ar":"اختر الكلمة الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"The wells dried up after three years with almost no rain. It was a terrible ___.","answer":"drought","explanation":"Drought.","explanation_ar":"جفاف.","options":["flood","climate","carbon","drought"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"After the heavy rain, water covered the road in the valley. There was a ___.","answer":"flood","explanation":"Flood.","explanation_ar":"سيل.","options":["drought","flood","resource","claim"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"The panels on our school roof turn sunlight into electricity. They are ___ panels.","answer":"solar","explanation":"Solar.","explanation_ar":"شمسية.","options":["global","carbon","valid","solar"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Smoke and gases from cars and factories are called ___.","answer":"emissions","explanation":"Emissions.","explanation_ar":"انبعاثات.","options":["resources","alternatives","emissions","droughts"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Fresh water, oil and good farmland are important ___.","answer":"resources","explanation":"Resource.","explanation_ar":"موارد.","options":["claims","floods","resources","emissions"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"A city that uses clean energy, saves water and protects nature for the future is ___.","answer":"sustainable","explanation":"Sustainable.","explanation_ar":"مستدامة.","options":["sustainable","nonstop","global","toxic"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS1-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Prefixes. Choose.","instructions_ar":"السوابق: اختر.","items":[{"q":"The prefix *re-* in *refill* means ___.","answer":"again","explanation":"re- = again.","explanation_ar":"مرة أخرى.","options":["not","to make","again","before"],"dok":1,"skill":"prefixes","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Oil cannot be replaced once we use it. It is a ___ resource.","answer":"nonrenewable","explanation":"non- = not.","explanation_ar":"غير متجدد.","options":["nonrenewable","renewable","unrenewable","rerenewable"],"dok":2,"skill":"prefixes","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Plastic in the sea can ___ turtles and dolphins.","answer":"endanger","explanation":"en- = put into.","explanation_ar":"يعرّض للخطر.","options":["danger","endanger","dangerous","nondanger"],"dok":2,"skill":"prefixes","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which word about community gardens is spelled correctly?","answer":"empower","explanation":"em- before p.","explanation_ar":"empower.","options":["empower","enpower","inpower","nonpower"],"dok":2,"skill":"spelling","stage":"guided"},{"q":"This paint is *nontoxic*. What does that mean?","answer":"It is not poisonous.","explanation":"non- + toxic.","explanation_ar":"غير سام.","options":["It is poisonous again.","It makes things poisonous.","It is very toxic.","It is not poisonous."],"dok":2,"skill":"prefixes","stage":"guided"},{"q":"In which sentence is *re* NOT a prefix?","answer":"I read an article about floods.","explanation":"Not a prefix.","explanation_ar":"read ليست سابقة.","options":["We reuse our glass jars.","I read an article about floods.","They rebuilt the wall after the flood.","Please refill the water tank."],"dok":3,"skill":"prefixes","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS1-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Use the word bank. Each word fits one sentence.","instructions_ar":"استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"The writer's main ________ is that plastic bags should be banned.","answer":"claim","explanation":"Statement.","explanation_ar":"ادعاء.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"A fact about turtles is ________ evidence for an essay about plastic in the sea.","answer":"relevant","explanation":"Connected.","explanation_ar":"ذات صلة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Your reason is ________ because it is supported by facts.","answer":"valid","explanation":"Acceptable.","explanation_ar":"سليم.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Cloth bags are a good ________ to plastic bags.","answer":"alternative","explanation":"Another choice.","explanation_ar":"بديل.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Solar panels ________ families to make their own electricity.","answer":"enable","explanation":"Make able.","explanation_ar":"تمكّن.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"We can ________ the amount of water we use by taking shorter showers.","answer":"reduce","explanation":"Make less.","explanation_ar":"نقلّل.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS1-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write with the new words.","instructions_ar":"اكتب باستخدام الكلمات الجديدة.","items":[{"q":"Explain the difference between *reduce*, *reuse* and *recycle* with one example each.","answer":"Reduce means use less: I take shorter showers. Reuse means use again: I refill my water bottle. Recycle means make into something new: we put cans in the recycling bin.","explanation":"3 Rs.","explanation_ar":"الثلاثة.","dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write two sentences: one with *renewable* and one with *nonrenewable*.","answer":"Sunlight is a renewable resource. Oil is a nonrenewable resource, so we should not waste it.","explanation":"Prefixes.","explanation_ar":"السوابق.","dok":2,"skill":"prefixes","stage":"production"},{"q":"What is one alternative to driving children to school every day? Why is it better for the environment?","answer":"Walking or taking the school bus is a good alternative, because it reduces car emissions.","explanation":"Alternative.","explanation_ar":"بديل.","dok":2,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which environmental problem is most serious in your city – drought, floods or pollution? Give one relevant reason.","answer":"Drought is the most serious problem in my city because we get very little rain and use a lot of water.","explanation":"Opinion + reason.","explanation_ar":"رأي وسبب.","dok":3,"skill":"reasoning","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Prefix Power Plant","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Power plant: the child drags prefixes onto base words; each real word sends electricity to a building in a green city, and the child must choose the right sentence for the word. Fake words cause a power cut.","Teams draw one prefix card and three base-word cards.","They build as many real words as they can and write their meanings.","For each word, they say a sentence about the environment.","The teacher colors one light bulb in the team's city for every correct word.","Real word = 1 point; correct meaning = 1 point; good sentence = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *the usual weather of a place over many years*?","answer":"climate","explanation":"Climate.","explanation_ar":"المناخ.","options":["climate","flood","carbon","resource"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is a *prefix*?","answer":"letters added to the beginning of a word to change its meaning","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"السابقة.","options":["letters added to the end of a word","letters added to the beginning of a word to change its meaning","a word with the same meaning","a kind of sentence"],"dok":1,"skill":"prefixes"},{"q":"*Wind is a **renewable** resource.* *Renewable* means ___.","answer":"able to be replaced naturally","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"متجدد.","options":["very expensive","dangerous","able to be replaced naturally","found only underground"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What does the prefix *non-* mean?","answer":"not","explanation":"non- = not.","explanation_ar":"ليس.","options":["not","again","to make","after"],"dok":1,"skill":"prefixes"},{"q":"Which sentence about recycling at school is correct?","answer":"Our class recycles paper every Thursday.","explanation":"Singular class → recycles.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Our class recycles paper every Thursday.","Our class recycle paper every Thursday.","Our class recycling paper every Thursday.","Our class are recycle paper every Thursday."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"*Plastic bags **endanger** camels in the desert.* What does *endanger* mean?","answer":"put in danger","explanation":"en- + danger.","explanation_ar":"يعرّض للخطر.","options":["rescue","put in danger","feed","count"],"dok":2,"skill":"prefixes"},{"q":"Which word means *use again*?","answer":"reuse","explanation":"re- = again.","explanation_ar":"reuse.","options":["nonuse","enuse","misuse","reuse"],"dok":2,"skill":"prefixes"},{"q":"Which reason is RELEVANT to the claim *Our school should plant more trees*?","answer":"Trees give shade and take in carbon dioxide.","explanation":"Relevant.","explanation_ar":"ذو صلة.","options":["Our school has a red door.","Trees give shade and take in carbon dioxide.","My brother likes football.","The canteen sells juice."],"dok":2,"skill":"relevant reasons"},{"q":"Using word parts, what does *enrich* the soil probably mean?","answer":"make the soil richer, with more food for plants","explanation":"en- = make.","explanation_ar":"يجعلها أغنى.","options":["take everything out of the soil","make the soil wet again","make the soil not rich","make the soil richer, with more food for plants"],"dok":3,"skill":"prefixes"},{"q":"Why is it helpful to know prefixes when you read science texts?","answer":"One prefix can help you understand many new words, like renewable and nonrenewable.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"تساعد على فهم كلمات كثيرة.","options":["Prefixes make texts shorter.","Prefixes are always at the end of words.","One prefix can help you understand many new words, like renewable and nonrenewable.","Prefixes only appear in poems."],"dok":3,"skill":"word strategies"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'climate': a globe with sun, rain and snow icons"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'environment': a green landscape with a river and animals"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'recycle': a recycling bin with arrows"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'renewable': a sun and a wind turbine"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'solar': solar panels on a roof"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'emissions': smoke from a car exhaust"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'global': a globe with people holding hands around it"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V08","prompt":"Picture for the word 'drought': cracked dry ground"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V09","prompt":"Picture for the word 'flood': water covering a street"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V10","prompt":"Picture for the word 'resource': a water drop, an oil barrel and a tree"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V11","prompt":"Picture for the word 'reduce': a big arrow becoming small"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V12","prompt":"Picture for the word 'reuse': a cloth bag used again and again"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V13","prompt":"Picture for the word 'carbon': a tree with CO₂ arrows going in"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V14","prompt":"Picture for the word 'sustainable': a green city with solar panels and trees"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V15","prompt":"Picture for the word 'claim': a speaker pointing at a sign"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V16","prompt":"Picture for the word 'relevant': a puzzle piece that fits"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V17","prompt":"Picture for the word 'valid': a check mark on a reason card"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V18","prompt":"Picture for the word 'alternative': a cloth bag next to a crossed-out plastic bag"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-01","prompt":"A dry landscape beside a green, sustainable city."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-02","prompt":"Icons for environment words."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS1-03","prompt":"Prefix blocks joining base words."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V01","script":"climate. The climate of Taif is cooler than Riyadh's."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V02","script":"environment. Plastic harms the environment."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V03","script":"recycle. We recycle cans and paper at school."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V04","script":"renewable. Solar power is a renewable energy."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V05","script":"solar. Solar panels turn sunlight into electricity."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V06","script":"emissions. Cars produce emissions."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V07","script":"global. Climate change is a global problem."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V08","script":"drought. The drought dried up the wells."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V09","script":"flood. Heavy rain caused a flood in the valley."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V10","script":"resource. Water is a precious resource."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V11","script":"reduce. Reduce the water you use."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V12","script":"reuse. Reuse your shopping bags."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V13","script":"carbon. Trees take in carbon dioxide."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V14","script":"sustainable. Saudi Arabia is building sustainable cities."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V15","script":"claim. The writer's claim is that plastic bags should be banned."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V16","script":"relevant. Give relevant evidence for your claim."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V17","script":"valid. That is a valid point."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-V18","script":"alternative. Cloth bags are a good alternative to plastic bags."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS1-VID","script":"Our planet is changing – and the words we use to talk about it can change things too! Climate, environment, recycle, renewable, solar, emissions, global, drought, flood, resource, reduce, reuse, carbon, sustainable! Make a claim, give relevant and valid reasons, and suggest an alternative! Re- means again. Non- means not. En- means to make or to put into – and it becomes em- before b and p! Find the prefix, find the base, join the meanings – and check the sentence!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS2","title":"Plastic Bags Should Be Banned","lesson_type":"close_reading","objectives":["read an opinion essay of about 460 words arguing that plastic bags should be banned","identify the writer's claim, reasons, evidence, counterargument and rebuttal","recognize persuasive techniques: facts and statistics, examples, rhetorical questions, emotional language and a call to action"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة مقال رأي من نحو 460 كلمة يدعو إلى حظر الأكياس البلاستيكية.","تحديد ادعاء الكاتبة وأسبابها وأدلتها والرأي المعارض والرد عليه.","التعرّف على أساليب الإقناع: الحقائق والإحصاءات والأمثلة والأسئلة البلاغية واللغة العاطفية والدعوة إلى العمل."],"success_criteria":["I can state the writer's claim and her three reasons in my own words.","I can find the evidence for each reason and the counterargument and rebuttal.","I can name a persuasive technique in the essay and explain its effect on the reader."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع ذكر ادعاء الكاتبة وأسبابها الثلاثة بكلماتي.","أستطيع إيجاد الدليل على كل سبب والرأي المعارض والرد عليه.","أستطيع تسمية أسلوب إقناع في المقال وشرح أثره في القارئ."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"ban","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/bæn/","definition":"to say officially that something is not allowed; an official rule that stops something","arabic":"يحظر / حظر","example":"Some countries ban plastic bags.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"single-use","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌsɪŋɡəl ˈjus/","definition":"made to be used only once and then thrown away","arabic":"للاستخدام مرة واحدة","example":"Single-use cups create a lot of waste.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"swallow","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈswɑloʊ/","definition":"to make food or drink go down your throat","arabic":"يبتلع","example":"The turtle swallowed a plastic bag.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"microplastics","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmaɪkroʊˌplæstɪks/","definition":"very small pieces of plastic, smaller than a grain of rice","arabic":"اللدائن الدقيقة","example":"Microplastics can end up in fish.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"convenient","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/kənˈvinjənt/","definition":"easy and useful for you","arabic":"مريح / عملي","example":"Plastic bags are cheap and convenient.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"counterargument","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkaʊntərˌɑrɡjəmənt/","definition":"a reason that someone on the other side might give","arabic":"الرأي المعارض / الحجة المضادة","example":"A strong essay answers the counterargument.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"rebuttal","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˈbʌtəl/","definition":"an answer that shows why the other side's argument is weaker","arabic":"الرد / التفنيد","example":"Her rebuttal mentions cloth bags.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"An **opinion essay** tries to persuade readers. Its **claim** (the writer's main opinion) is usually in the **introduction**. Each **body paragraph** gives one **reason**, supported by **evidence** – facts, statistics, examples or expert information. Strong essays also include a **counterargument** (what the other side says) and a **rebuttal** (why the writer still disagrees).\n\nWriters use **persuasive techniques**: **facts and statistics** (*Rwanda banned plastic bags in 2008*); **specific examples** (*turtles … camels*); **rhetorical questions** – questions that make the reader think, not answer (*Is this the picture of our country we want visitors to see?*); **emotional language** (*deadly, garbage dumps*); and a **call to action** (*take a reusable bag*).\n\nTo analyze an essay, map it: **Claim → Reason 1 + evidence → Reason 2 + evidence → Reason 3 + evidence → Counterargument → Rebuttal → Conclusion**. Notice how **headings** and **signal words** (*First, Second, Third, Some people argue, In conclusion*) guide you.","examples":["Claim: ***I believe that single-use plastic bags should be banned in Saudi Arabia.***","Reason 1: *They harm animals.* → Evidence: *turtles mistake floating bags for jellyfish; plastic bags in the stomachs of hundreds of camels.*","Reason 2: *They pollute for centuries.* → Evidence: *Sunlight slowly breaks them into tiny pieces called microplastics.*","Reason 3: *They waste resources.* → Evidence: *made from oil and natural gas … used for an average of only about twelve minutes.*","Counterargument: ***Some people argue that plastic bags are cheap and convenient.***","Rebuttal: ***This is a fair point, but there are better alternatives.***","Statistics / facts: *Rwanda (2008), Kenya (2017), Ireland (2002).*","Rhetorical question: ***Is this the picture of our country that we want visitors to see?***","Call to action: ***Next time you go shopping, take a reusable bag.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Confusing the topic with the claim. → ✓ Topic: plastic bags. Claim: plastic bags should be banned. (A claim is a full opinion.)","✗ Thinking the counterargument is the writer's opinion. → ✓ The counterargument is what OTHER people say. (Look for *Some people argue*.)","✗ Calling every question a rhetorical question. → ✓ A rhetorical question doesn't need an answer – it makes you think. (Check its purpose.)","✗ Plastic bags is harmful. → ✓ Plastic bags are harmful. (Plural → **are**.)","✗ Turtles mistake bags as jellyfish. → ✓ Turtles mistake bags for jellyfish. (mistake A **for** B.)","✗ Evidence: plastic is bad. → ✓ Evidence: plastic bags were found in hundreds of camels' stomachs. (Evidence = facts, examples, numbers.)","✗ Ignoring headings. → ✓ Use headings to find each reason quickly. (Headings guide you.)"],"arabic":"مقال الرأي يحاول إقناع القارئ، ويأتي الادعاء (الرأي الرئيسي) عادة في المقدمة، وتقدّم كل فقرة في المتن سببًا يدعمه دليل من حقائق أو إحصاءات أو أمثلة.\n\nالمقال القوي يذكر الرأي المعارض (ما يقوله الطرف الآخر) ثم يرد عليه موضحًا لماذا يبقى الكاتب على رأيه.\n\nأساليب الإقناع: الحقائق والإحصاءات، والأمثلة المحددة، والسؤال البلاغي الذي يدفع القارئ إلى التفكير، واللغة العاطفية، والدعوة إلى العمل.\n\nانتبه: الموضوع كلمة (الأكياس البلاستيكية)، أما الادعاء فرأي كامل (يجب حظرها). وتُقال mistake A for B."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Twelve minutes of use… hundreds of years of pollution. Is it worth it?","on_screen":"Plastic Bags Should Be Banned 🛍️🚫","visual":"A plastic bag drifts across a desert road and into the sea."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"First, plastic bags are deadly for animals… Second, they pollute for centuries… Third, they waste resources…","on_screen":"Reason 1 · Reason 2 · Reason 3","visual":"A turtle near a floating bag; camels in the desert; an oil rig and a bag."},{"time":"01:30–02:20","narration":"Some people argue that plastic bags are cheap and convenient. This is a fair point, but…","on_screen":"Counterargument → Rebuttal","visual":"Two speakers in a friendly debate."},{"time":"02:20–03:30","narration":"Facts, examples, rhetorical questions, emotional words and a call to action – the writer's persuasion toolkit!","on_screen":"Persuasion toolkit 🧰","visual":"Tools pop out of a toolbox, each labeled with a technique."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Map the argument: claim, reasons, evidence, counterargument, rebuttal, conclusion!","on_screen":"Argument Map","visual":"A map with arrows from the claim to each part."}],"captions_ar":["اثنتا عشرة دقيقة من الاستخدام… ومئات السنين من التلوث. هل يستحق ذلك؟","أولًا، الأكياس البلاستيكية قاتلة للحيوانات… ثانيًا، تلوّث لقرون… ثالثًا، تهدر الموارد…","يقول بعض الناس إن الأكياس البلاستيكية رخيصة وعملية. هذه نقطة عادلة، لكن…","الحقائق والأمثلة والأسئلة البلاغية والكلمات العاطفية والدعوة إلى العمل: صندوق أدوات الإقناع!","ارسم خريطة الحجة: الادعاء والأسباب والأدلة والرأي المعارض والرد والخاتمة!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, clear female voice, speed 1.0. A student reads Huda's essay."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Plastic Bags Should Be Banned","body":"*From the school magazine – by Huda, Grade 8*\n\nEvery day, millions of thin plastic bags carry our shopping home. We use each one for a short time, and then we throw it away. But that bag does not disappear. It can stay in our environment for hundreds of years. I believe that single-use plastic bags should be banned in Saudi Arabia, because they harm animals, pollute our land and sea for centuries, and waste valuable resources.\n\n**They Harm Animals**\n\nFirst, plastic bags are deadly for animals. In the sea, turtles often mistake floating bags for jellyfish, one of their favorite foods. Once a turtle swallows a bag, the plastic can block its stomach. The problem is not only in the ocean. Scientists in the United Arab Emirates have found large lumps of plastic bags in the stomachs of hundreds of camels that died in the desert. A camel cannot tell the difference between a plastic bag and food – but we can choose not to throw bags away.\n\n**They Pollute for Centuries**\n\nSecond, plastic bags do not break down like paper or food. Sunlight slowly breaks them into tiny pieces called microplastics, which can end up in soil, in water and even in the fish we eat. Bags also blow across the desert and get caught in bushes and fences, turning beautiful places into garbage dumps. Is this the picture of our country that we want visitors to see?\n\n**They Waste Resources**\n\nThird, most plastic bags are made from oil and natural gas, which are nonrenewable resources. These resources took millions of years to form. According to some estimates, a plastic bag is used for an average of only about twelve minutes. It makes little sense to turn a resource we can never replace into something we keep for less time than it takes to drink a cup of tea.\n\n**But Aren't Bags Useful?**\n\nSome people argue that plastic bags are cheap and convenient, and that many families reuse them as trash bags. This is a fair point, but there are better alternatives. Cloth bags can be used hundreds of times, and paper bags break down much faster. Countries that have acted have seen real results. Rwanda banned plastic bags in 2008, and Kenya did the same in 2017. When Ireland put a small charge on each plastic bag in 2002, people quickly started using far fewer of them.\n\n**A Small Change with a Big Impact**\n\nIn conclusion, single-use plastic bags hurt animals, pollute our land and sea for centuries, and waste resources we cannot replace. Banning them would be a small change with a huge impact. Next time you go shopping, take a reusable bag – and ask your family, your school and your city to do the same.","word_count":458,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U2-LS2-1","genre":"opinion/argument","role":"main","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[{"word":"single-use","definition":"made to be used only once and then thrown away","arabic":"للاستخدام مرة واحدة"},{"word":"microplastics","definition":"very small pieces of plastic, smaller than a grain of rice","arabic":"اللدائن الدقيقة (الميكروبلاستيك)"},{"word":"convenient","definition":"easy and useful for you","arabic":"مريح / عملي"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the essay. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ المقال واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Who wrote *Plastic Bags Should Be Banned*?","answer":"Huda, a Grade 8 student","explanation":"Byline.","explanation_ar":"هدى، طالبة في الصف الثامن.","options":["a scientist from the UAE","the Minister of Environment","a shop owner","Huda, a Grade 8 student"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What do sea turtles often mistake plastic bags for?","answer":"jellyfish","explanation":"They Harm Animals.","explanation_ar":"قناديل البحر.","options":["seaweed","jellyfish","small fish","coral"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Where did scientists find plastic bags in hundreds of camels' stomachs?","answer":"in the United Arab Emirates","explanation":"They Harm Animals.","explanation_ar":"في الإمارات.","options":["in the United Arab Emirates","in Kenya","in Ireland","in Rwanda"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What are *microplastics*?","answer":"tiny pieces of plastic that come from breaking bags","explanation":"They Pollute for Centuries.","explanation_ar":"قطع بلاستيكية صغيرة جدًا.","options":["new reusable bags","a kind of jellyfish","plastic factories","tiny pieces of plastic that come from breaking bags"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which country banned plastic bags in 2008?","answer":"Rwanda","explanation":"But Aren't Bags Useful?","explanation_ar":"رواندا.","options":["Kenya","Ireland","Rwanda","Saudi Arabia"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What does Huda ask readers to do at the end?","answer":"take a reusable bag when shopping and ask others to do the same","explanation":"Call to action.","explanation_ar":"حمل كيس قابل لإعادة الاستخدام.","options":["write to the newspaper","stop shopping","take a reusable bag when shopping and ask others to do the same","collect plastic bags"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS2-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Claim, reasons, evidence and techniques. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الادعاء والأسباب والأدلة والأساليب: اختر.","items":[{"q":"What is Huda's CLAIM?","answer":"Single-use plastic bags should be banned in Saudi Arabia.","explanation":"Claim.","explanation_ar":"الادعاء.","options":["Single-use plastic bags should be banned in Saudi Arabia.","Plastic bags are cheap.","Camels live in the desert.","Ireland charges for bags."],"dok":1,"skill":"claims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which evidence supports the reason *They waste resources*?","answer":"Most plastic bags are made from oil and natural gas and are used for only about twelve minutes.","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"مصنوعة من النفط وتُستخدم دقائق.","options":["Most plastic bags are made from oil and natural gas and are used for only about twelve minutes.","Turtles mistake bags for jellyfish.","Bags blow across the desert.","Kenya banned bags in 2017."],"dok":2,"skill":"reasons and evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence is the COUNTERARGUMENT in Huda's essay?","answer":"Some people argue that plastic bags are cheap and convenient.","explanation":"Counterargument.","explanation_ar":"الرأي المعارض.","options":["Plastic bags are deadly for animals.","Some people argue that plastic bags are cheap and convenient.","Banning them would be a small change with a huge impact.","Bags also blow across the desert."],"dok":2,"skill":"counterargument","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*Is this the picture of our country that we want visitors to see?* What kind of sentence is this?","answer":"a rhetorical question that makes readers think","explanation":"Technique.","explanation_ar":"سؤال بلاغي.","options":["a real question that needs an answer","a statistic","a rhetorical question that makes readers think","a counterargument"],"dok":2,"skill":"persuasive techniques","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why does Huda mention Rwanda, Kenya and Ireland?","answer":"to show that banning or charging for bags has worked in real countries","explanation":"Purpose of evidence.","explanation_ar":"لتُثبت أن الحظر نجح.","options":["to describe their deserts","to compare their animals","to show that banning or charging for bags has worked in real countries","to show that plastic bags are useful"],"dok":2,"skill":"reasons and evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which word in the essay is an example of EMOTIONAL language?","answer":"deadly","explanation":"Technique.","explanation_ar":"deadly.","options":["Second","estimates","Grade","deadly"],"dok":3,"skill":"persuasive techniques","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS2-A3","type":"highlight","stage":"freer","instructions":"Find the evidence: Find the evidence.","instructions_ar":"ابحث عن الدليل.","items":[{"q":"Underline the sentence where Huda answers the counterargument.","answer":"This is a fair point, but there are better alternatives.","explanation":"Rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"الرد.","evidence":"This is a fair point, but there are better alternatives.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L06-U2-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"rebuttal","stage":"freer","sentences":["*From the school magazine – by Huda, Grade 8*","Every day, millions of thin plastic bags carry our shopping home.","We use each one for a short time, and then we throw it away.","But that bag does not disappear.","It can stay in our environment for hundreds of years.","I believe that single-use plastic bags should be banned in Saudi Arabia, because they harm animals, pollute our land and sea for centuries, and waste valuable resources.","**They Harm Animals**","First, plastic bags are deadly for animals.","In the sea, turtles often mistake floating bags for jellyfish, one of their favorite foods.","Once a turtle swallows a bag, the plastic can block its stomach.","The problem is not only in the ocean.","Scientists in the United Arab Emirates have found large lumps of plastic bags in the stomachs of hundreds of camels that died in the desert.","A camel cannot tell the difference between a plastic bag and food – but we can choose not to throw bags away.","**They Pollute for Centuries**","Second, plastic bags do not break down like paper or food.","Sunlight slowly breaks them into tiny pieces called microplastics, which can end up in soil, in water and even in the fish we eat.","Bags also blow across the desert and get caught in bushes and fences, turning beautiful places into garbage dumps.","Is this the picture of our country that we want visitors to see?","**They Waste Resources**","Third, most plastic bags are made from oil and natural gas, which are nonrenewable resources.","These resources took millions of years to form.","According to some estimates, a plastic bag is used for an average of only about twelve minutes.","It makes little sense to turn a resource we can never replace into something we keep for less time than it takes to drink a cup of tea.","**But Aren't Bags Useful?**","Some people argue that plastic bags are cheap and convenient, and that many families reuse them as trash bags.","This is a fair point, but there are better alternatives.","Cloth bags can be used hundreds of times, and paper bags break down much faster.","Countries that have acted have seen real results.","Rwanda banned plastic bags in 2008, and Kenya did the same in 2017.","When Ireland put a small charge on each plastic bag in 2002, people quickly started using far fewer of them.","**A Small Change with a Big Impact**","In conclusion, single-use plastic bags hurt animals, pollute our land and sea for centuries, and waste resources we cannot replace.","Banning them would be a small change with a huge impact.","Next time you go shopping, take a reusable bag – and ask your family, your school and your city to do the same."]},{"q":"Underline the sentence that explains what happens to bags in sunlight.","answer":"Sunlight slowly breaks them into tiny pieces called microplastics, which can end up in soil, in water and even in the fish we eat.","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"الدليل.","evidence":"Sunlight slowly breaks them into tiny pieces called microplastics, which can end up in soil, in water and even in the fish we eat.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L06-U2-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"reasons and evidence","stage":"freer","sentences":["*From the school magazine – by Huda, Grade 8*","Every day, millions of thin plastic bags carry our shopping home.","We use each one for a short time, and then we throw it away.","But that bag does not disappear.","It can stay in our environment for hundreds of years.","I believe that single-use plastic bags should be banned in Saudi Arabia, because they harm animals, pollute our land and sea for centuries, and waste valuable resources.","**They Harm Animals**","First, plastic bags are deadly for animals.","In the sea, turtles often mistake floating bags for jellyfish, one of their favorite foods.","Once a turtle swallows a bag, the plastic can block its stomach.","The problem is not only in the ocean.","Scientists in the United Arab Emirates have found large lumps of plastic bags in the stomachs of hundreds of camels that died in the desert.","A camel cannot tell the difference between a plastic bag and food – but we can choose not to throw bags away.","**They Pollute for Centuries**","Second, plastic bags do not break down like paper or food.","Sunlight slowly breaks them into tiny pieces called microplastics, which can end up in soil, in water and even in the fish we eat.","Bags also blow across the desert and get caught in bushes and fences, turning beautiful places into garbage dumps.","Is this the picture of our country that we want visitors to see?","**They Waste Resources**","Third, most plastic bags are made from oil and natural gas, which are nonrenewable resources.","These resources took millions of years to form.","According to some estimates, a plastic bag is used for an average of only about twelve minutes.","It makes little sense to turn a resource we can never replace into something we keep for less time than it takes to drink a cup of tea.","**But Aren't Bags Useful?**","Some people argue that plastic bags are cheap and convenient, and that many families reuse them as trash bags.","This is a fair point, but there are better alternatives.","Cloth bags can be used hundreds of times, and paper bags break down much faster.","Countries that have acted have seen real results.","Rwanda banned plastic bags in 2008, and Kenya did the same in 2017.","When Ireland put a small charge on each plastic bag in 2002, people quickly started using far fewer of them.","**A Small Change with a Big Impact**","In conclusion, single-use plastic bags hurt animals, pollute our land and sea for centuries, and waste resources we cannot replace.","Banning them would be a small change with a huge impact.","Next time you go shopping, take a reusable bag – and ask your family, your school and your city to do the same."]},{"q":"Why does Huda compare twelve minutes with *the time it takes to drink a cup of tea*?","answer":"to make the short time easy to picture and show how wasteful it is","explanation":"Author's craft.","explanation_ar":"لتوضيح قِصر المدة.","options":["to advertise tea","to give a recipe","to explain how bags are made","to make the short time easy to picture and show how wasteful it is"],"dok":3,"skill":"persuasive techniques","stage":"freer"},{"q":"How do the headings help the reader?","answer":"Each heading names one reason or part of the argument, so readers can follow it.","explanation":"Text features.","explanation_ar":"تسمّي كل سبب.","options":["They tell a story.","Each heading names one reason or part of the argument, so readers can follow it.","They list the writer's friends.","They give the date."],"dok":2,"skill":"text features","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS2-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Analyze the argument.","instructions_ar":"حلّل الحجة.","items":[{"q":"Write Huda's claim and her three reasons in your own words.","answer":"Huda thinks Saudi Arabia should ban single-use plastic bags because they kill animals, stay in nature for a very long time and use up oil and gas that cannot be replaced.","explanation":"Claim + reasons.","explanation_ar":"الادعاء والأسباب.","dok":2,"skill":"summarizing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which of Huda's three reasons is the strongest? Explain using her evidence.","answer":"Reason 1 is the strongest because she gives specific examples – turtles and hundreds of camels – that show real harm.","explanation":"Evaluate.","explanation_ar":"قيّم.","dok":3,"skill":"evaluating reasons","stage":"production"},{"q":"Find two persuasive techniques in the essay and write an example of each.","answer":"Rhetorical question: Is this the picture of our country that we want visitors to see? Call to action: Next time you go shopping, take a reusable bag.","explanation":"Techniques.","explanation_ar":"الأساليب.","dok":2,"skill":"persuasive techniques","stage":"production"},{"q":"Do you agree with Huda? Give one reason and one piece of evidence from the essay.","answer":"Yes, I agree, because bags harm animals – scientists found plastic bags in hundreds of camels' stomachs.","explanation":"Opinion.","explanation_ar":"رأي.","dok":3,"skill":"reasoning","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS2-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"Plastic Bags Should Be Banned\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “Plastic Bags Should Be Banned”.","answer":"458 words","title":"Plastic Bags Should Be Banned","body":"*From the school magazine – by Huda, Grade 8*\n\nEvery day, millions of thin plastic bags carry our shopping home. We use each one for a short time, and then we throw it away. But that bag does not disappear. It can stay in our environment for hundreds of years. I believe that single-use plastic bags should be banned in Saudi Arabia, because they harm animals, pollute our land and sea for centuries, and waste valuable resources.\n\n**They Harm Animals**\n\nFirst, plastic bags are deadly for animals. In the sea, turtles often mistake floating bags for jellyfish, one of their favorite foods. Once a turtle swallows a bag, the plastic can block its stomach. The problem is not only in the ocean. Scientists in the United Arab Emirates have found large lumps of plastic bags in the stomachs of hundreds of camels that died in the desert. A camel cannot tell the difference between a plastic bag and food – but we can choose not to throw bags away.\n\n**They Pollute for Centuries**\n\nSecond, plastic bags do not break down like paper or food. Sunlight slowly breaks them into tiny pieces called microplastics, which can end up in soil, in water and even in the fish we eat. Bags also blow across the desert and get caught in bushes and fences, turning beautiful places into garbage dumps. Is this the picture of our country that we want visitors to see?\n\n**They Waste Resources**\n\nThird, most plastic bags are made from oil and natural gas, which are nonrenewable resources. These resources took millions of years to form. According to some estimates, a plastic bag is used for an average of only about twelve minutes. It makes little sense to turn a resource we can never replace into something we keep for less time than it takes to drink a cup of tea.\n\n**But Aren't Bags Useful?**\n\nSome people argue that plastic bags are cheap and convenient, and that many families reuse them as trash bags. This is a fair point, but there are better alternatives. Cloth bags can be used hundreds of times, and paper bags break down much faster. Countries that have acted have seen real results. Rwanda banned plastic bags in 2008, and Kenya did the same in 2017. When Ireland put a small charge on each plastic bag in 2002, people quickly started using far fewer of them.\n\n**A Small Change with a Big Impact**\n\nIn conclusion, single-use plastic bags hurt animals, pollute our land and sea for centuries, and waste resources we cannot replace. Banning them would be a small change with a huge impact. Next time you go shopping, take a reusable bag – and ask your family, your school and your city to do the same.","words":458,"limit":253}],"time_limit_sec":253},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS2-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Argument Map","instructions":"Organizer: Map Huda's essay: write her claim, each reason with its evidence, the counterargument and her rebuttal.","items":[{"q":"Claim","answer":"Single-use plastic bags should be banned in Saudi Arabia."},{"q":"Reason 1 + evidence","answer":"They harm animals – turtles mistake bags for jellyfish; plastic found in hundreds of camels' stomachs."},{"q":"Reason 2 + evidence","answer":"They pollute for centuries – sunlight breaks them into microplastics that end up in soil, water and fish."},{"q":"Reason 3 + evidence","answer":"They waste resources – made from oil and gas; used for only about twelve minutes on average."},{"q":"Counterargument","answer":"Plastic bags are cheap and convenient, and families reuse them as trash bags."},{"q":"Rebuttal","answer":"There are better alternatives (cloth and paper bags), and bans in Rwanda, Kenya and Ireland worked."}]}],"game":{"name":"Argument Architects","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Argument builder: the child drags sentence blocks into a building – claim on top, reasons and evidence as pillars, the rebuttal as the roof support. Labeling each technique correctly adds solar panels to the building.","Each team receives the mixed sentence strips.","They place the claim at the top, then each reason with its evidence below it.","They add the counterargument and rebuttal, then the conclusion.","They label two persuasive techniques.","Correct part = 1 point; correct technique label = 1 bonus point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *claim* in an opinion essay?","answer":"the writer's main opinion","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الرأي الرئيسي.","options":["a fact with a number","the title only","a question for the reader","the writer's main opinion"],"dok":1,"skill":"claims"},{"q":"What does *ban* mean?","answer":"to officially stop something from being allowed","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"يحظر.","options":["to buy something","to recycle something","to make something cheaper","to officially stop something from being allowed"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Plastic bags are cheap and **convenient**.* *Convenient* means ___.","answer":"easy and useful","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"عملية.","options":["dangerous","easy and useful","expensive","heavy"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"In an opinion essay like Huda's, what is a *counterargument*?","answer":"a reason that people on the other side might give","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الرأي المعارض.","options":["a reason that people on the other side might give","the writer's strongest reason","a statistic","the conclusion"],"dok":1,"skill":"counterargument"},{"q":"Which sentence about turtles uses *mistake … for* correctly?","answer":"Turtles mistake bags for jellyfish.","explanation":"mistake A for B.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Turtles mistake bags as jellyfish.","Turtles mistake bags to jellyfish.","Turtles mistake bags for jellyfish.","Turtles mistakes bags for jellyfish."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which detail from the essay is a STATISTIC or dated fact?","answer":"Kenya banned plastic bags in 2017.","explanation":"Facts and statistics.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة مؤرخة.","options":["Plastic bags are deadly.","This is a fair point.","Kenya banned plastic bags in 2017.","We can choose not to throw bags away."],"dok":2,"skill":"persuasive techniques"},{"q":"What is the purpose of the rebuttal in Huda's essay?","answer":"to show why the other side's argument is weaker","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"إضعاف الرأي المعارض.","options":["to agree completely with the other side","to change the topic","to tell a story","to show why the other side's argument is weaker"],"dok":2,"skill":"rebuttal"},{"q":"Which reason is NOT relevant to Huda's claim about plastic bags?","answer":"Plastic bags come in many colors.","explanation":"Relevance.","explanation_ar":"غير ذي صلة.","options":["Plastic bags harm animals.","Plastic bags pollute for centuries.","Plastic bags come in many colors.","Plastic bags waste resources."],"dok":2,"skill":"relevant reasons"},{"q":"Why does Huda include a counterargument instead of ignoring it?","answer":"Answering it makes her argument more convincing and fair.","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"يجعل حجتها أقوى وأعدل.","options":["Answering it makes her argument more convincing and fair.","She is not sure of her claim.","She wants a longer title.","She agrees that bags are great."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating arguments"},{"q":"Which evidence would make Huda's Reason 2 even STRONGER?","answer":"a study showing how many microplastics were found in fish in the Red Sea","explanation":"Strong evidence.","explanation_ar":"دراسة عن البلاستيك في أسماك البحر الأحمر.","options":["a photo of her cat","a study showing how many microplastics were found in fish in the Red Sea","the price of tea","the name of her school"],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating evidence"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'ban': a plastic bag with a red 'no' sign"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'single-use': a pile of plastic cups and straws"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'swallow': a turtle with a bag near its mouth"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'microplastics': tiny colored pieces on a fingertip"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'convenient': a shopper carrying bags easily"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'counterargument': two speech bubbles facing each other"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'rebuttal': a speaker answering politely with a raised finger"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-01","prompt":"Plastic bags harming desert and sea animals."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-02","prompt":"An argument map for an opinion essay."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS2-03","prompt":"A toolbox of persuasive techniques."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V01","script":"ban. Some countries ban plastic bags."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V02","script":"single-use. Single-use cups create a lot of waste."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V03","script":"swallow. The turtle swallowed a plastic bag."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V04","script":"microplastics. Microplastics can end up in fish."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V05","script":"convenient. Plastic bags are cheap and convenient."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V06","script":"counterargument. A strong essay answers the counterargument."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-V07","script":"rebuttal. Her rebuttal mentions cloth bags."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-T1","script":"*From the school magazine – by Huda, Grade 8* Every day, millions of thin plastic bags carry our shopping home. We use each one for a short time, and then we throw it away. But that bag does not disappear. It can stay in our environment for hundreds of years. I believe that single-use plastic bags should be banned in Saudi Arabia, because they harm animals, pollute our land and sea for centuries, and waste valuable resources. **They Harm Animals** First, plastic bags are deadly for animals. In the sea, turtles often mistake floating bags for jellyfish, one of their favorite foods. Once a turtle swallows a bag, the plastic can block its stomach. The problem is not only in the ocean. Scientists in the United Arab Emirates have found large lumps of plastic bags in the stomachs of hundreds of camels that died in the desert. A camel cannot tell the difference between a plastic bag and food – but we can choose not to throw bags away. **They Pollute for Centuries** Second, plastic bags do not break down like paper or food. Sunlight slowly breaks them into tiny pieces called microplastics, which can end up in soil, in water and even in the fish we eat. Bags also blow across the desert and get caught in bushes and fences, turning beautiful places into garbage dumps. Is this the picture of our country that we want visitors to see? **They Waste Resources** Third, most plastic bags are made from oil and natural gas, which are nonrenewable resources. These resources took millions of years to form. According to some estimates, a plastic bag is used for an average of only about twelve minutes. It makes little sense to turn a resource we can never replace into something we keep for less time than it takes to drink a cup of tea. **But Aren't Bags Useful?** Some people argue that plastic bags are cheap and convenient, and that many families reuse them as trash bags. This is a fair point, but there are better alternatives. Cloth bags can be used hundreds of times, and paper bags break down much faster. Countries that have acted have seen real results. Rwanda banned plastic bags in 2008, and Kenya did the same in 2017. When Ireland put a small charge on each plastic bag in 2002, people quickly started using far fewer of them. **A Small Change with a Big Impact** In conclusion, single-use plastic bags hurt animals, pollute our land and sea for centuries, and waste resources we cannot replace. Banning them would be a small change with a huge impact. Next time you go shopping, take a reusable bag – and ask your family, your school and your city to do the same."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS2-VID","script":"Twelve minutes of use… hundreds of years of pollution. Is it worth it? First, plastic bags are deadly for animals… Second, they pollute for centuries… Third, they waste resources… Some people argue that plastic bags are cheap and convenient. This is a fair point, but… Facts, examples, rhetorical questions, emotional words and a call to action – the writer's persuasion toolkit! Map the argument: claim, reasons, evidence, counterargument, rebuttal, conclusion!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS3","title":"Evaluating reasons and evidence: \"How Solar Farms Work\"","lesson_type":"strategy_paired_text","objectives":["evaluate whether a writer's reasons are relevant and whether the evidence is sufficient and reliable","separate supported claims from unsupported opinions in an informational text and an opinion essay","suggest stronger evidence for a weak claim"],"objectives_ar":["تقييم مدى صلة أسباب الكاتب بالادعاء وكفاية الأدلة وموثوقيتها.","التمييز بين الادعاءات المدعومة والآراء غير المدعومة في نص معلوماتي ومقال رأي.","اقتراح دليل أقوى لادعاء ضعيف."],"success_criteria":["I can check a reason with three questions: Is it relevant? Is there enough evidence? Is the source reliable?","I can find one well-supported claim and one unsupported claim in each text.","I can explain how to make a weak claim stronger."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع فحص السبب بثلاثة أسئلة: هل له صلة؟ هل الدليل كافٍ؟ هل المصدر موثوق؟","أستطيع إيجاد ادعاء مدعوم وآخر غير مدعوم في كل نص.","أستطيع شرح كيفية تقوية ادعاء ضعيف."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"panel","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpænəl/","definition":"a flat piece of material; a flat board that collects sunlight","arabic":"لوح","example":"The roof is covered with solar panels.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"electron","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪˈlɛkˌtrɑn/","definition":"a very tiny particle; moving electrons make electricity","arabic":"إلكترون","example":"Sunlight makes electrons move.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"current","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkɝənt/","definition":"a flow of electricity (or water)","arabic":"تيار","example":"Moving electrons make an electric current.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"reliable","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/rɪˈlaɪəbəl/","definition":"able to be trusted","arabic":"موثوق","example":"A government science website is a reliable source.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"sufficient","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/səˈfɪʃənt/","definition":"enough for a purpose","arabic":"كافٍ","example":"One example is not sufficient evidence.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"supporter","pos":"noun","ipa":"/səˈpɔrtər/","definition":"a person who agrees with and helps an idea or a team","arabic":"مؤيّد","example":"Supporters of solar power want more solar farms.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"challenge","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtʃælɪndʒ/","definition":"a difficult problem that needs effort to solve","arabic":"تحدٍّ","example":"Dust is a challenge for desert solar farms.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"Good readers don't believe every claim. They **evaluate** it – they judge how **strong** it is. Ask three questions about each reason and its evidence: (1) **Relevant?** Is it really connected to the claim? (2) **Sufficient?** Is there **enough** evidence, or only one small example? (3) **Reliable?** Does it come from a **trustworthy source** – scientists, a government agency, a respected organization – or from an unnamed person or an advertisement?\n\nA **supported claim** has relevant, sufficient and reliable evidence: *According to the United States Department of Energy, the sunlight … in about an hour and a half … could power the whole world for a year.* An **unsupported claim** has no evidence, or only opinion words: *Supporters say that solar energy is the best energy in the world.*\n\nWatch for **hedge words** that show uncertainty: *some estimates, may, might, about*. They are honest, but they tell you the evidence is not exact. To make a weak claim stronger, add **facts, numbers, expert sources or more examples**.","examples":["Strong (reliable source): *According to the United States Department of Energy…* → a government science agency.","Strong (relevant fact): *Panels make no electricity at night, so energy must be stored in large batteries.*","Strong (scientists): Huda: *Scientists in the United Arab Emirates have found large lumps of plastic bags in … hundreds of camels.*","Unsupported opinion: *Supporters say that solar energy is the best energy in the world.* → no evidence; *best* = judgment.","Hedged evidence: Huda: *According to some estimates, a plastic bag is used for … about twelve minutes.* → honest but not exact.","Emotional, not evidence: Huda: *turning beautiful places into garbage dumps* → creates a feeling, but no numbers.","Not relevant: *Solar panels are blue.* → does not support *solar farms are good for Saudi Arabia*.","Not sufficient: *My uncle's roof has panels, so all homes should have them.* → only one example.","Make it stronger: add *how much* electricity Sakaka produces, or *how many* homes it powers."],"common_errors":["✗ Believing a claim because it sounds confident. → ✓ Check the evidence first. (Confident ≠ true.)","✗ Thinking one example proves everything. → ✓ One example is not sufficient. (Look for enough evidence.)","✗ Trusting any source the same way. → ✓ Prefer scientists, governments and respected organizations. (Check reliability.)","✗ Calling emotional language 'evidence'. → ✓ Evidence = facts, numbers, expert findings. (Feelings ≠ proof.)","✗ A evidence is weak. → ✓ The evidence is weak. / A piece of evidence is weak. (evidence = uncountable.)","✗ The source is reliability. → ✓ The source is reliable. (After *is* → adjective.)","✗ Ignoring hedge words. → ✓ Notice *some estimates, about, may*. (Hedges show uncertainty.)"],"arabic":"القارئ الجيد لا يصدّق كل ادعاء، بل يقيّمه بثلاثة أسئلة: هل السبب ذو صلة؟ هل الدليل كافٍ؟ هل المصدر موثوق؟\n\nالادعاء المدعوم له دليل ذو صلة وكافٍ وموثوق، أما غير المدعوم فلا دليل له أو يعتمد على كلمات الرأي فقط مثل best.\n\nانتبه لكلمات التحفّظ مثل some estimates وabout وmay؛ فهي أمينة لكنها تدل على أن الدليل غير دقيق. ولتقوية الادعاء أضف حقائق وأرقامًا ومصادر خبيرة وأمثلة أكثر.\n\nانتبه: evidence اسم غير معدود، فلا نقول an evidence بل a piece of evidence."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"An hour and a half of sunlight could power the world for a year. Is that true? Who says so?","on_screen":"How Solar Farms Work ☀️⚡","visual":"Sunrise over rows of solar panels in the Saudi desert."},{"time":"00:30–01:40","narration":"Sunlight hits silicon cells, electrons move, inverters change DC to AC, and power lines carry it home.","on_screen":"Sun → cells → electrons → inverter → home","visual":"An animated path from the sun to a house."},{"time":"01:40–02:30","narration":"Night, dust and storage are real challenges – and robots clean panels without water!","on_screen":"Challenges","visual":"A night sky over panels, a dusty panel, a robot gliding along a row."},{"time":"02:30–03:30","narration":"Relevant? Sufficient? Reliable? Three questions to test any claim.","on_screen":"✔ Relevant · ✔ Sufficient · ✔ Reliable","visual":"A claim goes through three checkpoints like airport security."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"'The best energy in the world' – where is the evidence? Ask for proof!","on_screen":"Opinion without evidence ✗","visual":"A claim balloon pops when it meets the 'evidence?' needle."}],"captions_ar":["ساعة ونصف من ضوء الشمس قد تكفي لتزويد العالم بالطاقة لعام كامل. هل هذا صحيح؟ ومن يقول ذلك؟","يسقط الضوء على خلايا السيليكون فتتحرك الإلكترونات، ثم يحوّل العاكس التيار، وتنقله الخطوط إلى البيوت.","الليل والغبار والتخزين تحديات حقيقية، وهناك روبوتات تنظف الألواح دون ماء!","هل له صلة؟ هل هو كافٍ؟ هل هو موثوق؟ ثلاثة أسئلة لاختبار أي ادعاء.","«أفضل طاقة في العالم» – أين الدليل؟ اطلب البرهان!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, calm male science-reporter voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"How Solar Farms Work","body":"According to the United States Department of Energy, the sunlight that reaches Earth's surface in about an hour and a half carries enough energy to power the whole world for a year. A solar farm is a large area of land covered with rows of solar panels that collect some of this energy and turn it into electricity.\n\n**Sunlight to Electricity** – Each panel is made of many solar cells, which are usually made from silicon, a material found in sand. When sunlight hits a cell, it makes tiny particles called electrons move. Moving electrons are an electric current.\n\n**From the Farm to Your Home** – The electricity from the panels is direct current (DC). Machines called inverters change it into alternating current (AC), the kind we use at home. Then power lines carry it to cities and towns.\n\n**Why the Desert?** – Saudi Arabia is one of the sunniest countries in the world, and it has huge areas of flat, open land. The Sakaka solar plant in Al-Jouf was one of the country's first large solar farms. Saudi Arabia plans to produce about half of its electricity from renewable sources, such as solar and wind, by 2030.\n\n**Challenges** – Solar farms also have problems. Panels make no electricity at night, so energy must be stored in large batteries or come from other power stations. Desert dust is another problem, because a dusty panel produces less power. Some solar farms use robots that clean the panels at night without using any water.\n\n**The Future** – Supporters say that solar energy is the best energy in the world. Meanwhile, scientists are working to make panels cheaper and batteries stronger, so that sunshine can power more of our lives.","word_count":282,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U2-LS3-1","genre":"informational","role":"paired","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[{"word":"silicon","definition":"a hard material found in sand and rocks, used to make solar cells and computer chips","arabic":"السيليكون"},{"word":"inverter","definition":"a machine that changes direct current (DC) into alternating current (AC)","arabic":"العاكس (محوّل التيار)"},{"word":"power grid","definition":"the network of power lines that carries electricity to homes","arabic":"شبكة الكهرباء"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read *How Solar Farms Work*. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ المقال واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What is a solar farm?","answer":"a large area of land covered with rows of solar panels","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"أرض واسعة مغطاة بألواح شمسية.","options":["a farm that grows sunflowers","a factory that makes batteries","a large area of land covered with rows of solar panels","a building for cooling homes"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What material are most solar cells made from?","answer":"silicon, which is found in sand","explanation":"Sunlight to Electricity.","explanation_ar":"السيليكون.","options":["plastic","silicon, which is found in sand","glass only","iron"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What do inverters do?","answer":"change direct current (DC) into alternating current (AC)","explanation":"From the Farm to Your Home.","explanation_ar":"تحوّل التيار المستمر إلى متردد.","options":["clean the panels","store energy at night","make the panels move","change direct current (DC) into alternating current (AC)"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Where is the Sakaka solar plant?","answer":"in Al-Jouf","explanation":"Why the Desert?","explanation_ar":"في الجوف.","options":["in Jeddah","in Al-Jouf","in Al-Ahsa","in Abha"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What problem do solar panels have at night?","answer":"They make no electricity.","explanation":"Challenges.","explanation_ar":"لا تنتج كهرباء.","options":["They make no electricity.","They get too hot.","They become dusty.","They change into AC."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How do some solar farms keep their panels clean?","answer":"Robots clean them at night without using water.","explanation":"Challenges.","explanation_ar":"روبوتات تنظفها دون ماء.","options":["Robots clean them at night without using water.","Workers wash them with seawater.","Rain cleans them every day.","They are never cleaned."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS3-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Evaluate reasons and evidence. Choose.","instructions_ar":"قيّم الأسباب والأدلة: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Why is the claim about one and a half hours of sunlight well supported?","answer":"It comes from a reliable source: the United States Department of Energy.","explanation":"Reliable source.","explanation_ar":"مصدر موثوق.","options":["It uses the word *best*.","It is in the last paragraph.","It is about robots.","It comes from a reliable source: the United States Department of Energy."],"dok":2,"skill":"evaluating evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which claim in the solar article has NO supporting evidence?","answer":"Solar energy is the best energy in the world.","explanation":"Unsupported.","explanation_ar":"غير مدعوم.","options":["Panels make no electricity at night.","Solar energy is the best energy in the world.","Inverters change DC into AC.","Silicon is found in sand."],"dok":2,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which fact is RELEVANT to the claim *The desert is a good place for solar farms*?","answer":"Saudi Arabia is one of the sunniest countries and has huge areas of flat land.","explanation":"Relevance.","explanation_ar":"ذو صلة.","options":["Panels are made of many cells.","Saudi Arabia is one of the sunniest countries and has huge areas of flat land.","Robots can be noisy.","Inverters are machines."],"dok":2,"skill":"relevant reasons","stage":"guided"},{"q":"In Huda's essay, why is *about twelve minutes* weaker evidence than the camel study?","answer":"It is hedged with *some estimates*, so it is not exact.","explanation":"Hedge words.","explanation_ar":"متحفَّظ عليه.","options":["It is about turtles.","It comes from scientists.","It is hedged with *some estimates*, so it is not exact.","It is in the conclusion."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A student writes: *My cousin's school has solar panels, so every school in the world should have them.* What is the problem?","answer":"One example is not sufficient evidence for such a big claim.","explanation":"Sufficient?","explanation_ar":"مثال واحد غير كافٍ.","options":["One example is not sufficient evidence for such a big claim.","The evidence is not relevant to schools.","The source is the Department of Energy.","The sentence is too short."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which source would be MOST reliable for facts about microplastics in the Red Sea?","answer":"a study by marine scientists at a Saudi university","explanation":"Reliability.","explanation_ar":"دراسة علماء بحار.","options":["a post by an unknown person online","an advertisement for plastic bags","a study by marine scientists at a Saudi university","a cartoon about fish"],"dok":2,"skill":"reliable sources","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS3-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Complete the Evidence Checker.","instructions_ar":"أكمل مدقق الأدلة.","items":[{"q":"Write one strong claim from the solar article and explain why it is strong.","answer":"The sun's energy in about an hour and a half could power the world for a year. It is strong because it comes from the US Department of Energy, a reliable source.","explanation":"Strong claim.","explanation_ar":"ادعاء قوي.","dok":2,"skill":"evaluating evidence","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write one unsupported claim from the solar article and explain what is missing.","answer":"Supporters say solar energy is the best energy in the world. There is no evidence, and *best* is an opinion word.","explanation":"Weak claim.","explanation_ar":"ادعاء ضعيف.","dok":2,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write one strong and one weaker piece of evidence from Huda's essay.","answer":"Strong: scientists found plastic bags in hundreds of camels' stomachs. Weaker: about twelve minutes of use, based on 'some estimates'.","explanation":"Huda's essay.","explanation_ar":"مقال هدى.","dok":3,"skill":"evaluating evidence","stage":"freer"},{"q":"How could the writer make the 'best energy' claim stronger?","answer":"Add facts comparing solar with oil power: its cost, its emissions and how much electricity it makes, from a reliable source.","explanation":"Improve.","explanation_ar":"حسّن.","dok":3,"skill":"evaluating claims","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS3-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Compare and evaluate.","instructions_ar":"قارن وقيّم.","items":[{"q":"Which text uses stronger evidence overall – Huda's essay or the solar article? Explain.","answer":"The solar article is stronger overall because most claims are facts from reliable sources, while Huda mixes facts with emotional language.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"production"},{"q":"How are the purposes of Huda's essay and the solar article different?","answer":"Huda's essay tries to persuade readers to ban plastic bags; the solar article mainly informs readers how solar farms work.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"الغرض.","dok":2,"skill":"author's purpose","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain one challenge of solar farms and one solution from the article.","answer":"Panels make no electricity at night, so energy is stored in large batteries.","explanation":"Problem–solution.","explanation_ar":"مشكلة وحل.","dok":2,"skill":"key details","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a claim about renewable energy in your city and support it with one relevant, reliable piece of evidence.","answer":"My city should build more solar panels, because according to the article Saudi Arabia is one of the sunniest countries in the world.","explanation":"Create.","explanation_ar":"أنشئ.","dok":3,"skill":"supporting claims","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS3-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"How Solar Farms Work\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “How Solar Farms Work”.","answer":"282 words","title":"How Solar Farms Work","body":"According to the United States Department of Energy, the sunlight that reaches Earth's surface in about an hour and a half carries enough energy to power the whole world for a year. A solar farm is a large area of land covered with rows of solar panels that collect some of this energy and turn it into electricity.\n\n**Sunlight to Electricity** – Each panel is made of many solar cells, which are usually made from silicon, a material found in sand. When sunlight hits a cell, it makes tiny particles called electrons move. Moving electrons are an electric current.\n\n**From the Farm to Your Home** – The electricity from the panels is direct current (DC). Machines called inverters change it into alternating current (AC), the kind we use at home. Then power lines carry it to cities and towns.\n\n**Why the Desert?** – Saudi Arabia is one of the sunniest countries in the world, and it has huge areas of flat, open land. The Sakaka solar plant in Al-Jouf was one of the country's first large solar farms. Saudi Arabia plans to produce about half of its electricity from renewable sources, such as solar and wind, by 2030.\n\n**Challenges** – Solar farms also have problems. Panels make no electricity at night, so energy must be stored in large batteries or come from other power stations. Desert dust is another problem, because a dusty panel produces less power. Some solar farms use robots that clean the panels at night without using any water.\n\n**The Future** – Supporters say that solar energy is the best energy in the world. Meanwhile, scientists are working to make panels cheaper and batteries stronger, so that sunshine can power more of our lives.","words":282,"limit":162}],"time_limit_sec":162},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS3-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Evidence Checker","instructions":"Organizer: Choose claims from both texts. Check each with the three questions and decide how to make a weak claim stronger.","items":[{"q":"Solar article – a strong claim and its evidence","answer":"The sun's energy in about an hour and a half could power the world for a year – from the US Department of Energy."},{"q":"Why it is strong (relevant? sufficient? reliable?)","answer":"Relevant to solar power; a reliable government science agency; a clear fact."},{"q":"Solar article – a weak or unsupported claim","answer":"Supporters say solar energy is the best energy in the world – no evidence, opinion word *best*."},{"q":"Huda's essay – a strong claim and its evidence","answer":"Plastic bags harm animals – scientists found plastic in hundreds of camels' stomachs."},{"q":"Huda's essay – a weaker piece of evidence and why","answer":"About twelve minutes of use – hedged with *some estimates*, so it is not exact."},{"q":"How to make one weak claim stronger","answer":"Add facts: how much electricity solar farms make, their costs and their effects compared with oil power stations."}]}],"game":{"name":"Evidence Inspectors","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Inspection line: claims roll along a conveyor belt; the child scans each with three buttons (relevant? sufficient? reliable?) and stamps it STRONG, WEAK or NO EVIDENCE. Correct stamps power up a solar farm.","The teacher reads a claim card and its evidence.","Teams discuss the three questions for 30 seconds.","Teams raise a green (strong), yellow (weak) or red (no evidence) paddle.","One team explains its rating; others can challenge.","Correct rating = 2 points; clear explanation = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What does it mean to *evaluate* evidence?","answer":"to judge how strong and trustworthy it is","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"تقييم قوة الدليل.","options":["to copy it","to judge how strong and trustworthy it is","to make it longer","to translate it"],"dok":1,"skill":"evaluating evidence"},{"q":"What does *reliable* mean?","answer":"able to be trusted","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"موثوق.","options":["very old","able to be trusted","very short","funny"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*One example is not **sufficient** evidence.* *Sufficient* means ___.","answer":"enough","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"كافٍ.","options":["relevant","exciting","false","enough"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which question checks if evidence is RELEVANT?","answer":"Is it really connected to the claim?","explanation":"Relevant.","explanation_ar":"هل له صلة؟","options":["Is it long enough?","Is it in color?","Is it really connected to the claim?","Is it in the title?"],"dok":1,"skill":"evaluating evidence"},{"q":"Which sentence about evidence is correct?","answer":"The evidence is strong.","explanation":"evidence = uncountable.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The evidence is strong.","The evidences are strong.","An evidence is strong.","The evidence are strong."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which is the MOST reliable source for facts about solar energy?","answer":"a government energy department","explanation":"Reliability.","explanation_ar":"جهة حكومية للطاقة.","options":["an unnamed comment online","an advertisement for a car","a friend's guess","a government energy department"],"dok":2,"skill":"reliable sources"},{"q":"What do the words *some estimates* and *about* tell you?","answer":"The evidence is not exact.","explanation":"Hedge words.","explanation_ar":"الدليل غير دقيق.","options":["The evidence is not exact.","The evidence is from scientists.","The claim is an opinion.","The text is a story."],"dok":2,"skill":"evaluating evidence"},{"q":"Why can't solar panels make electricity all day and night?","answer":"They need sunlight, and there is none at night.","explanation":"Cause.","explanation_ar":"تحتاج إلى ضوء الشمس.","options":["They are switched off by robots.","Inverters stop them.","Silicon melts at night.","They need sunlight, and there is none at night."],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect"},{"q":"Which claim about electric cars has the STRONGEST support?","answer":"A government study found that electric cars in our city reduced emissions by 20% last year.","explanation":"Strong evidence.","explanation_ar":"دراسة حكومية برقم.","options":["Electric cars are the coolest cars ever.","My neighbor likes his electric car.","A government study found that electric cars in our city reduced emissions by 20% last year.","Electric cars come in many colors."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating evidence"},{"q":"Why should readers evaluate evidence instead of believing every claim?","answer":"Some claims are opinions or use weak evidence, and readers need to decide what is true.","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"لأن بعض الادعاءات آراء أو أدلتها ضعيفة.","options":["Some claims are opinions or use weak evidence, and readers need to decide what is true.","All claims are false.","Evaluating makes reading faster.","Writers never use facts."],"dok":3,"skill":"critical reading"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'panel': rows of blue solar panels"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'electron': small dots moving along a wire"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'current': arrows moving along a wire to a bulb"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'reliable': a shield with a check mark"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'sufficient': a scale balanced with enough weights"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'supporter': people holding a 'Go Solar' sign"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'challenge': a dusty solar panel"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-01","prompt":"A desert solar farm with a cleaning robot."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-02","prompt":"Diagram of solar electricity reaching a home."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS3-03","prompt":"Three checkpoints for evaluating evidence."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V01","script":"panel. The roof is covered with solar panels."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V02","script":"electron. Sunlight makes electrons move."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V03","script":"current. Moving electrons make an electric current."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V04","script":"reliable. A government science website is a reliable source."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V05","script":"sufficient. One example is not sufficient evidence."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V06","script":"supporter. Supporters of solar power want more solar farms."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-V07","script":"challenge. Dust is a challenge for desert solar farms."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-T1","script":"According to the United States Department of Energy, the sunlight that reaches Earth's surface in about an hour and a half carries enough energy to power the whole world for a year. A solar farm is a large area of land covered with rows of solar panels that collect some of this energy and turn it into electricity. **Sunlight to Electricity** – Each panel is made of many solar cells, which are usually made from silicon, a material found in sand. When sunlight hits a cell, it makes tiny particles called electrons move. Moving electrons are an electric current. **From the Farm to Your Home** – The electricity from the panels is direct current (DC). Machines called inverters change it into alternating current (AC), the kind we use at home. Then power lines carry it to cities and towns. **Why the Desert?** – Saudi Arabia is one of the sunniest countries in the world, and it has huge areas of flat, open land. The Sakaka solar plant in Al-Jouf was one of the country's first large solar farms. Saudi Arabia plans to produce about half of its electricity from renewable sources, such as solar and wind, by 2030. **Challenges** – Solar farms also have problems. Panels make no electricity at night, so energy must be stored in large batteries or come from other power stations. Desert dust is another problem, because a dusty panel produces less power. Some solar farms use robots that clean the panels at night without using any water. **The Future** – Supporters say that solar energy is the best energy in the world. Meanwhile, scientists are working to make panels cheaper and batteries stronger, so that sunshine can power more of our lives."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS3-VID","script":"An hour and a half of sunlight could power the world for a year. Is that true? Who says so? Sunlight hits silicon cells, electrons move, inverters change DC to AC, and power lines carry it home. Night, dust and storage are real challenges – and robots clean panels without water! Relevant? Sufficient? Reliable? Three questions to test any claim. 'The best energy in the world' – where is the evidence? Ask for proof!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS4","title":"Writing Skill: Opinion essay structure","lesson_type":"writing_skill","objectives":["identify the parts of a three-paragraph opinion essay: introduction with a thesis, body with reasons and a counterargument, and a conclusion","write a thesis statement that gives an opinion and previews three reasons","write a counterargument and a rebuttal with *Some people say … However, …*"],"objectives_ar":["تحديد أجزاء مقال الرأي من ثلاث فقرات: مقدمة بأطروحة، ومتن بالأسباب والرأي المعارض، وخاتمة.","كتابة أطروحة تعرض الرأي وتمهّد لثلاثة أسباب.","كتابة رأي معارض والرد عليه باستخدام Some people say … However."],"success_criteria":["My thesis statement has a clear opinion and three reasons in the same order as my body.","My body paragraph uses transitions and gives evidence for each reason.","I include a fair counterargument and a strong rebuttal."],"success_criteria_ar":["أطروحتي فيها رأي واضح وثلاثة أسباب بالترتيب نفسه في المتن.","فقرة المتن تستخدم كلمات الانتقال وتقدّم دليلًا لكل سبب.","أعرض رأيًا معارضًا بإنصاف وأرد عليه ردًا قويًا."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"thesis statement","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈθisɪs ˌsteɪtmənt/","definition":"the sentence in the introduction that gives the writer's main opinion","arabic":"الأطروحة / جملة الفكرة الرئيسة","example":"My thesis statement is the last sentence of my introduction.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"preview","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/ˈpriˌvju/","definition":"to give a short look at what is coming next","arabic":"يمهّد / لمحة مسبقة","example":"The thesis previews three reasons.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"transition","pos":"noun","ipa":"/trænˈzɪʃən/","definition":"a word or phrase that connects ideas, such as *First* or *However*","arabic":"كلمة انتقالية / رابط","example":"Use transitions to connect your reasons.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"landfill","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlændˌfɪl/","definition":"a place where rubbish is buried under the ground","arabic":"مكب النفايات","example":"Most of our rubbish goes to a landfill.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"eco-monitor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈikoʊ ˌmɑnɪtər/","definition":"a student whose job is to help the class protect the environment","arabic":"مراقب بيئي","example":"The eco-monitor empties the recycling bins.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"habit","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhæbɪt/","definition":"something you do often, often without thinking","arabic":"عادة","example":"Recycling is a good habit.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"however","pos":"adverb","ipa":"/haʊˈɛvər/","definition":"a word that introduces a different or opposite idea","arabic":"ومع ذلك / لكن","example":"However, small bins fit in one corner.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **three-paragraph opinion essay** has a clear plan. **Paragraph 1 – Introduction:** a **hook** + a little **background** + the **thesis statement**: your opinion **and a preview of your reasons** (*I believe that…, because…, … and …*). The thesis is usually the **last sentence** of the introduction.\n\n**Paragraph 2 – Body:** your **reasons in the same order** as the preview, each introduced by a **transition** (*First, Second, Finally*) and supported by **evidence** or an **example**. Then a **counterargument** (*Some people say that…*) and a **rebuttal** (*However, …*). **Paragraph 3 – Conclusion:** restate the thesis **in new words**, **sum up** the reasons and end with a **call to action** or a strong final thought.\n\nTips: one paragraph = one job; keep reasons **relevant** and **different** from each other; be **fair** to the other side, then show why your view is still stronger.","examples":["Hook: ***Every day, our school throws away bags full of paper, cans and plastic bottles.***","Thesis with preview: ***I believe that every classroom should have a recycling station, because it would reduce waste, build good habits and even raise money for our school.***","Reason 1 + evidence: ***First, recycling stations would reduce waste. Our class alone fills a large box with used paper every week.***","Reason 2: ***Second, students would build good habits that they can take home to their families.***","Reason 3: ***Finally, the school could sell clean paper and cans … and use the money for library books.***","Counterargument: ***Some people say that recycling stations would take up space and make classrooms messy.***","Rebuttal: ***However, three small, labeled bins fit in one corner.***","Conclusion – restate + sum up: ***Recycling stations would help our school waste less, learn more and earn money.***","Call to action: ***Let's ask our principal to start this term!***"],"common_errors":["✗ A thesis with no reasons: I like recycling. → ✓ I believe that…, because…, … and …. (Opinion + preview.)","✗ Reasons in a different order from the preview. → ✓ Keep the same order in the body. (Match the preview.)","✗ Two reasons that say the same thing. → ✓ Choose three different reasons. (Different reasons.)","✗ Ignoring the other side. → ✓ Add Some people say… However, …. (Be fair, then rebut.)","✗ Some people say that bins is messy. → ✓ Some people say that bins are messy. (Plural → **are**.)","✗ However bins fit in a corner. → ✓ However, bins fit in a corner. (Comma after **However**.)","✗ A conclusion that copies the thesis. → ✓ Restate it in new words. (Restate, don't repeat.)"],"arabic":"مقال الرأي من ثلاث فقرات له خطة واضحة: المقدمة فيها جملة جذب وخلفية قصيرة ثم الأطروحة (رأيك مع لمحة عن أسبابك)، وتكون عادة آخر جملة في المقدمة.\n\nالمتن: أسبابك بالترتيب نفسه الوارد في الأطروحة، يسبق كلًّا منها كلمة انتقالية (First, Second, Finally) ويدعمه دليل أو مثال، ثم الرأي المعارض (Some people say that) والرد عليه (However).\n\nالخاتمة: أعد صياغة الأطروحة بكلمات جديدة، ولخّص الأسباب، واختم بدعوة إلى العمل أو فكرة أخيرة قوية.\n\nانتبه: ضع فاصلة بعد However، واجعل أسبابك مختلفة وذات صلة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"An opinion essay is like a building: a strong roof, solid pillars and a firm base!","on_screen":"Opinion Essay Structure 🏗️","visual":"A three-floor building labeled Introduction, Body, Conclusion."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Every day, our school throws away bags full of paper, cans and plastic bottles…","on_screen":"(model essay)","visual":"Mazen with three small recycling bins in a classroom corner."},{"time":"01:30–02:30","narration":"The thesis gives your opinion AND previews your reasons – in the order you'll explain them.","on_screen":"Thesis = opinion + preview","visual":"Three reason cards fly out of the thesis and land in the body in order."},{"time":"02:30–03:30","narration":"Some people say… However… Be fair to the other side, then show why you're still right.","on_screen":"Counterargument → Rebuttal","visual":"Two characters shake hands, then one points to the bins."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Restate, sum up and call to action – your essay ends strong!","on_screen":"Conclusion = restate + sum up + act","visual":"The building's top floor lights up."}],"captions_ar":["مقال الرأي كالمبنى: سقف قوي وأعمدة متينة وأساس ثابت!","كل يوم ترمي مدرستنا أكياسًا مليئة بالورق والعلب والقوارير البلاستيكية…","الأطروحة تعرض رأيك وتمهّد لأسبابك بالترتيب الذي ستشرحها به.","يقول بعض الناس… ومع ذلك… كن منصفًا للطرف الآخر ثم بيّن لماذا يبقى رأيك أقوى.","أعد الصياغة ولخّص وادعُ إلى العمل، فتنتهي مقالتك بقوة!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, friendly male teacher voice, speed 1.0. Mazen reads his essay."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Every Classroom Should Have a Recycling Station (by Mazen)","body":"Every day, our school throws away bags full of paper, cans and plastic bottles, and most of it ends up in a landfill outside the city. I believe that every classroom should have a recycling station, because it would reduce waste, build good habits and even raise money for our school.\n\nFirst, recycling stations would reduce waste. Our class alone fills a large box with used paper every week, and all of it could be recycled instead of buried. Second, students would build good habits that they can take home to their families. Finally, the school could sell clean paper and cans to a recycling company and use the money for library books. Some people say that recycling stations would take up space and make classrooms messy. However, three small, labeled bins fit in one corner, and student 'eco-monitors' could empty them every afternoon.\n\nIn conclusion, recycling stations would help our school waste less, learn more and earn money. They are a small step toward a cleaner, more sustainable Saudi Arabia. Let's ask our principal to start this term!","word_count":179,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U2-LS4-1","genre":"model text","role":"model","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[],"annotations":["**Introduction:** hook (*bags full of paper…*) + **thesis statement** with a **preview** of three reasons (*reduce waste, build good habits … raise money*).","**Body:** each reason starts with a **transition** (*First, Second, Finally*) and has **evidence** or an **example** (*fills a large box with used paper every week*).","**Counterargument:** *Some people say that…* shows the other side fairly.","**Rebuttal:** *However, …* answers it with a practical solution (small bins, eco-monitors).","**Conclusion:** restates the thesis in new words, sums up the reasons and ends with a **call to action**.","**Order of reasons:** the essay previews the reasons in the same order it explains them."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Mazen's essay. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ مقال مازن واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What is Mazen's thesis?","answer":"Every classroom should have a recycling station.","explanation":"Thesis.","explanation_ar":"يجب أن يكون في كل صف محطة تدوير.","options":["Every classroom should have a recycling station.","Landfills are outside the city.","Paper is heavy.","Library books are expensive."],"dok":1,"skill":"thesis","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which three reasons does Mazen preview?","answer":"reduce waste, build good habits and raise money","explanation":"Preview.","explanation_ar":"تقليل النفايات وبناء العادات وجمع المال.","options":["save time, make friends and play games","clean the floor, open windows and plant trees","reduce waste, build good habits and raise money","buy bins, paint walls and hire workers"],"dok":1,"skill":"thesis","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What evidence does Mazen give for Reason 1?","answer":"His class fills a large box with used paper every week.","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"صندوق كبير من الورق أسبوعيًا.","options":["His class fills a large box with used paper every week.","His family recycles at home.","Bins are cheap.","The principal likes recycling."],"dok":1,"skill":"reasons and evidence","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What would the school do with the money from recycling?","answer":"buy library books","explanation":"Reason 3.","explanation_ar":"شراء كتب للمكتبة.","options":["build a new gym","buy library books","buy plastic bags","pay the eco-monitors"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which transition introduces Mazen's last reason?","answer":"Finally,","explanation":"Transition.","explanation_ar":"Finally.","options":["First,","However,","Some people say","Finally,"],"dok":1,"skill":"transitions","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Where is Mazen's thesis statement?","answer":"at the end of the introduction","explanation":"Position.","explanation_ar":"في نهاية المقدمة.","options":["at the start of the conclusion","in the middle of the body","at the end of the introduction","in the title only"],"dok":2,"skill":"essay structure","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS4-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Opinion essay structure. Choose.","instructions_ar":"بنية مقال الرأي: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the BEST thesis statement for an essay about school water refill stations?","answer":"I believe our school should install water refill stations, because they would reduce plastic waste, save families money and keep students healthy.","explanation":"Thesis + preview.","explanation_ar":"أفضل أطروحة.","options":["Water is important.","I like water.","I believe our school should install water refill stations, because they would reduce plastic waste, save families money and keep students healthy.","Refill stations are blue."],"dok":2,"skill":"thesis","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A thesis previews *reduce waste, save money and build habits*. Which order should the body use?","answer":"reduce waste → save money → build habits","explanation":"Same order.","explanation_ar":"الترتيب نفسه.","options":["build habits → reduce waste → save money","reduce waste → save money → build habits","save money → build habits → reduce waste","any order at all"],"dok":2,"skill":"essay structure","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence is a COUNTERARGUMENT for an essay supporting tree planting at school?","answer":"Some people say that trees need too much water in our dry climate.","explanation":"Counterargument.","explanation_ar":"رأي معارض.","options":["Some people say that trees need too much water in our dry climate.","Trees give shade in the playground.","In conclusion, trees make our school greener.","First, trees clean the air."],"dok":2,"skill":"counterargument","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is the STRONGEST rebuttal to *Trees need too much water*?","answer":"However, desert trees like the ghaf need very little water once they grow.","explanation":"Rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"أقوى رد.","options":["However, I like trees.","However, water is wet.","However, some trees are tall.","However, desert trees like the ghaf need very little water once they grow."],"dok":3,"skill":"rebuttal","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about recycling bins uses *However* correctly?","answer":"Some people say bins are messy. However, labeled bins keep the room tidy.","explanation":"However + comma.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Some people say bins are messy however labeled bins keep the room tidy.","Some people say bins are messy. However, labeled bins keep the room tidy.","However some people say, bins are messy.","Some people say bins are messy, however. Labeled bins keep the room tidy."],"dok":2,"skill":"punctuation","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which job does the BODY paragraph of an opinion essay do?","answer":"gives reasons with evidence and answers the counterargument","explanation":"Body.","explanation_ar":"يقدّم الأسباب والأدلة ويرد على المعارض.","options":["only gives the hook","only gives the call to action","lists the writer's hobbies","gives reasons with evidence and answers the counterargument"],"dok":2,"skill":"essay structure","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS4-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Complete the essay with the word bank.","instructions_ar":"أكمل المقال من بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"I ________ that our city should have more bicycle lanes.","answer":"believe","explanation":"Opinion.","explanation_ar":"أعتقد.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________, bicycle lanes would reduce traffic.","answer":"First","explanation":"Transition.","explanation_ar":"أولًا.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Some people ________ that it is too hot to ride bicycles in summer.","answer":"say","explanation":"Counterargument.","explanation_ar":"يقولون.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________, people could ride in the cool early morning and evening.","answer":"However","explanation":"Rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"ومع ذلك.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"In ________, bicycle lanes would make our city healthier and cleaner.","answer":"conclusion","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الختام.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________ ask our city council to build them!","answer":"Let's","explanation":"Call to action.","explanation_ar":"لنطلب.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS4-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Plan and write parts of an opinion essay.","instructions_ar":"خطط لأجزاء مقال رأي واكتبها.","items":[{"q":"Write a thesis with a preview: *Should our school turn off the air conditioning in empty classrooms?*","answer":"I believe that our school should turn off the air conditioning in empty classrooms, because it would save electricity, reduce emissions and lower the school's bills.","explanation":"Thesis.","explanation_ar":"الأطروحة.","dok":2,"skill":"thesis","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one reason with evidence or an example for your thesis.","answer":"First, it would save electricity. Our classroom is empty for about an hour every day during break and PE, but the AC keeps running.","explanation":"Reason + evidence.","explanation_ar":"سبب ودليل.","dok":2,"skill":"reasons and evidence","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a counterargument and a rebuttal for your thesis.","answer":"Some people say that the room will be too hot when students return. However, the AC can be switched on five minutes before the lesson ends.","explanation":"Counter + rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"رأي معارض ورد.","dok":3,"skill":"counterargument","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a conclusion for your essay (restate, sum up, call to action).","answer":"In conclusion, switching off the AC in empty rooms would save power, cut emissions and save money. Let's make 'last one out, switch it off' our class rule!","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":3,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Essay Builders","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Essay tower: the child stacks sentence blocks into three floors (introduction, body, conclusion). One block is missing; the child writes it from a choice of sentence starters. A complete tower lights up with solar panels.","Each team opens an envelope and labels every strip.","They build the three paragraphs in order.","They discover the missing part and write it on the blank strip.","Teams read their essays aloud.","Correct order = 3 points; good missing part = 2 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *thesis statement*?","answer":"the sentence that gives the writer's main opinion","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الأطروحة.","options":["the last word of the essay","a question for the reader","the title","the sentence that gives the writer's main opinion"],"dok":1,"skill":"thesis"},{"q":"Where is the thesis usually found in a three-paragraph opinion essay?","answer":"at the end of the introduction","explanation":"Position.","explanation_ar":"نهاية المقدمة.","options":["at the end of the introduction","at the end of the conclusion","in the middle of the body","before the title"],"dok":1,"skill":"essay structure"},{"q":"*Most of our rubbish goes to a **landfill**.* A *landfill* is ___.","answer":"a place where rubbish is buried","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"مكب النفايات.","options":["a recycling machine","a farm","a place where rubbish is buried","a park"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which word is a transition?","answer":"Second,","explanation":"Transition.","explanation_ar":"Second.","options":["Second,","bottle","recycle","classroom"],"dok":1,"skill":"transitions"},{"q":"Which rebuttal sentence about the AC rule is punctuated correctly?","answer":"However, the room cools down again in five minutes.","explanation":"Comma after However.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["However the room cools down again in five minutes.","However, the room cools down again in five minutes.","However the room, cools down again in five minutes.","However; the room cools down again in five minutes."],"dok":2,"skill":"punctuation"},{"q":"What does a *preview* in a thesis statement do?","answer":"It lists the reasons the essay will explain.","explanation":"Preview.","explanation_ar":"يذكر الأسباب القادمة.","options":["It ends the essay.","It tells a story.","It lists the reasons the essay will explain.","It asks the reader a question."],"dok":2,"skill":"thesis"},{"q":"Why should the body follow the same order as the preview?","answer":"so readers can follow the argument easily","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"ليسهل على القارئ المتابعة.","options":["so the essay is longer","so readers can follow the argument easily","so the counterargument comes first","so the hook is repeated"],"dok":2,"skill":"essay structure"},{"q":"Which phrase usually introduces a counterargument?","answer":"Some people say that…","explanation":"Signal.","explanation_ar":"Some people say that.","options":["In conclusion,…","Some people say that…","First,…","Let's…"],"dok":2,"skill":"counterargument"},{"q":"A writer's three reasons are *saves money*, *saves cash* and *costs less*. What is the problem?","answer":"All three reasons say the same thing.","explanation":"Different reasons.","explanation_ar":"الأسباب متكررة.","options":["The reasons are too different.","There is no thesis.","All three reasons say the same thing.","The reasons are in the wrong order."],"dok":3,"skill":"reasons and evidence"},{"q":"Why does a strong opinion essay include a counterargument and a rebuttal?","answer":"It shows the writer is fair and makes the argument more convincing.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر الإنصاف ويقوّي الحجة.","options":["It makes the writer change sides.","It replaces the evidence.","It is only needed in stories.","It shows the writer is fair and makes the argument more convincing."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating arguments"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'thesis statement': a highlighted sentence in a paragraph"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'preview': a movie trailer on a screen"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'transition': a bridge between two islands"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'landfill': trucks dumping rubbish at a large site"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'eco-monitor': a student with a green badge and a bin"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'habit': a child putting a can in a recycling bin"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'however': a road sign with a curving arrow"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-01","prompt":"A classroom recycling station."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-02","prompt":"An essay shown as a three-floor building."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS4-03","prompt":"A thesis statement previewing three reasons."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V01","script":"thesis statement. My thesis statement is the last sentence of my introduction."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V02","script":"preview. The thesis previews three reasons."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V03","script":"transition. Use transitions to connect your reasons."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V04","script":"landfill. Most of our rubbish goes to a landfill."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V05","script":"eco-monitor. The eco-monitor empties the recycling bins."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V06","script":"habit. Recycling is a good habit."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-V07","script":"however. However, small bins fit in one corner."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-T1","script":"Every day, our school throws away bags full of paper, cans and plastic bottles, and most of it ends up in a landfill outside the city. I believe that every classroom should have a recycling station, because it would reduce waste, build good habits and even raise money for our school. First, recycling stations would reduce waste. Our class alone fills a large box with used paper every week, and all of it could be recycled instead of buried. Second, students would build good habits that they can take home to their families. Finally, the school could sell clean paper and cans to a recycling company and use the money for library books. Some people say that recycling stations would take up space and make classrooms messy. However, three small, labeled bins fit in one corner, and student 'eco-monitors' could empty them every afternoon. In conclusion, recycling stations would help our school waste less, learn more and earn money. They are a small step toward a cleaner, more sustainable Saudi Arabia. Let's ask our principal to start this term!"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS4-VID","script":"An opinion essay is like a building: a strong roof, solid pillars and a firm base! Every day, our school throws away bags full of paper, cans and plastic bottles… The thesis gives your opinion AND previews your reasons – in the order you'll explain them. Some people say… However… Be fair to the other side, then show why you're still right. Restate, sum up and call to action – your essay ends strong!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS5","title":"Writing Workshop: A three-paragraph opinion essay on an environmental rule","lesson_type":"writing_workshop","objectives":["plan a three-paragraph opinion essay supporting an environmental rule with the Opinion Essay Planner","write 150–200 words with a thesis and preview, three reasons with evidence, a counterargument and rebuttal, and a conclusion","revise, edit and present the essay at a class 'Green Council' meeting"],"objectives_ar":["التخطيط لمقال رأي من ثلاث فقرات يدعم قاعدة بيئية.","كتابة 150–200 كلمة بأطروحة وتمهيد وثلاثة أسباب بأدلة ورأي معارض ورد وخاتمة.","تحسين المقال وتدقيقه وتقديمه في اجتماع «المجلس الأخضر» الصفي."],"success_criteria":["My introduction has a hook and a thesis that previews three reasons.","My body gives three reasons with relevant evidence, plus a fair counterargument and a strong rebuttal.","My conclusion restates my thesis and ends with a call to action (150–200 words)."],"success_criteria_ar":["مقدمتي فيها جملة جذب وأطروحة تمهّد لثلاثة أسباب.","المتن فيه ثلاثة أسباب بأدلة ذات صلة ورأي معارض منصف ورد قوي.","خاتمتي تعيد صياغة الأطروحة وتنتهي بدعوة إلى العمل (150–200 كلمة)."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"engine","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɛndʒən/","definition":"the part of a car that burns fuel to make it move","arabic":"محرّك","example":"Switch off the engine while you wait.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"exhaust","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪɡˈzɔst/","definition":"the gas that comes out of a car's engine","arabic":"عادم","example":"Car exhaust pollutes the air.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"fuel","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfjuəl/","definition":"something burned to make heat or power, such as petrol","arabic":"وقود","example":"Waiting with the engine on wastes fuel.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"desalination","pos":"noun","ipa":"/diˌsæləˈneɪʃən/","definition":"removing salt from seawater to make fresh water","arabic":"تحلية المياه","example":"Desalination uses a lot of energy.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"native","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈneɪtɪv/","definition":"growing or living naturally in a place","arabic":"محلي / أصيل","example":"The ghaf is a native tree of Arabia.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"canteen","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kænˈtin/","definition":"a place in a school where students buy food and drinks","arabic":"مقصف","example":"The canteen sells juice and sandwiches.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"council","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkaʊnsəl/","definition":"a group of people who meet to make decisions","arabic":"مجلس","example":"Our class Green Council voted for the rule.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"Your essay will **persuade** readers to support an **environmental rule**. Choose the rule you can support with the **strongest evidence**. Use the **facts** on the rule card, and add an **example** from your own life if it is relevant.\n\n**Paragraph 1:** a **hook** + a **thesis** with a **preview** of three reasons. **Paragraph 2:** *First, … Second, … Finally, …* – each reason with **evidence** – then *Some people say that …* (**counterargument**) and *However, …* (**rebuttal**). **Paragraph 3:** *In conclusion, …* – restate the thesis in new words, sum up and end with a **call to action**.\n\nMake it **convincing**: use **relevant, reliable** facts; use **should** for the rule; answer the other side **politely** with a practical solution; keep it **150–200 words**.","examples":["Hook: ***Every afternoon, dozens of cars wait at our school gate with their engines running, filling the air with exhaust.***","Thesis + preview: ***I believe that drivers should switch off their engines while they wait, because it would protect our health, reduce emissions and save fuel.***","Reason 1 + evidence: ***First, it would protect students' health. Children breathe faster than adults, so they take in more polluted air for their size.***","Reason 2 + evidence: ***Second, an engine that is running but not moving still burns fuel and releases harmful gases.***","Reason 3: ***Finally, families would save money on fuel.***","Counterargument: ***Some people say that cars get too hot without the air conditioning on.***","Rebuttal: ***However, drivers could park in the shade, open the windows or start the engine a minute before their children arrive.***","Conclusion: ***In conclusion, a 'switch it off' rule would give us cleaner air, a healthier planet and fuller fuel tanks.***","Call to action: ***Let's put a friendly sign at our gate and ask every driver to switch off!***"],"common_errors":["✗ A thesis that does not name the rule. → ✓ I believe that drivers should switch off their engines… (Name the rule.)","✗ Reasons with no evidence. → ✓ Add a fact from the rule card. (Reason + evidence.)","✗ A rude rebuttal: People who say that are wrong. → ✓ However, drivers could park in the shade… (Polite and practical.)","✗ Drivers should switching off. → ✓ Drivers should switch off. (should + base verb.)","✗ Mixing two rules in one essay. → ✓ Write about ONE rule. (One rule only.)","✗ Forgetting the counterargument. → ✓ Add Some people say… However, …. (Answer the other side.)","✗ Writing 260 words. → ✓ Keep it 150–200 words. (Check the length.)"],"arabic":"ستكتب مقالًا يقنع القراء بدعم قاعدة بيئية، فاختر القاعدة التي تملك أقوى الأدلة عليها، واستخدم الحقائق في البطاقة، وأضف مثالًا من حياتك إن كان ذا صلة.\n\nالفقرة الأولى: جملة جذب وأطروحة تمهّد لثلاثة أسباب. الثانية: First وSecond وFinally مع دليل لكل سبب، ثم الرأي المعارض (Some people say that) والرد (However). الثالثة: In conclusion مع إعادة صياغة الأطروحة والتلخيص والدعوة إلى العمل.\n\nاجعل مقالك مقنعًا: حقائق ذات صلة وموثوقة، وshould للقاعدة، ورد مهذب بحل عملي، وطول 150–200 كلمة.\n\nانتبه: بعد should يأتي الفعل المجرد، واكتب عن قاعدة واحدة فقط."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"One small rule can make a big difference. Which rule will YOU fight for?","on_screen":"My Environmental Rule Essay 🌱","visual":"Four rule cards flip over: engines, straws, showers, trees."},{"time":"00:30–01:20","narration":"Read the facts – and the other side – before you choose.","on_screen":"Facts + Other side","visual":"Fact icons and a 'But…' speech bubble appear on each card."},{"time":"01:20–02:30","narration":"Every afternoon, dozens of cars wait at our school gate with their engines running…","on_screen":"(model essay)","visual":"Cars at a school gate; exhaust clouds fade as engines switch off; children breathe fresh air."},{"time":"02:30–03:30","narration":"Hook and thesis. Three reasons with evidence. Some people say… However… In conclusion… Let's…!","on_screen":"P1 · P2 · P3","visual":"Three paragraph blocks build a green tower."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Present your essay at the Green Council – and maybe your rule will become real!","on_screen":"Green Council 🏛️🌿","visual":"Students vote with green cards."}],"captions_ar":["قاعدة صغيرة قد تُحدث فرقًا كبيرًا. أي قاعدة ستدافع عنها أنت؟","اقرأ الحقائق ورأي الطرف الآخر قبل أن تختار.","كل ظهيرة تنتظر عشرات السيارات عند بوابة مدرستنا ومحركاتها تعمل…","جملة جذب وأطروحة، ثلاثة أسباب بأدلة، يقول بعض الناس… ومع ذلك… في الختام… لنفعل…!","قدّم مقالك في المجلس الأخضر، فربما تصبح قاعدتك حقيقة!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, upbeat female voice, speed 1.0. A student reads the model essay."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Environmental Rule Cards","body":"**Rule A – Drivers should switch off their engines while waiting at the school gate.** · Facts: an engine that is running but not moving still burns fuel and releases exhaust gases · children breathe faster than adults, so they take in more air – and more pollution – for their size · some cities around the world fine drivers who leave engines running while parked · Other side: in summer, cars get hot without the air conditioning\n\n**Rule B – Our school canteen should stop using plastic straws and cups.** · Facts: straws and cups are often among the items found in beach clean-ups · plastic straws are light and small, so many recycling machines cannot sort them · alternatives: paper straws, steel straws, reusable cups · Other side: reusable cups must be washed\n\n**Rule C – Families should take showers of five minutes or less.** · Facts: an ordinary shower can use about 10 liters of water every minute · Saudi Arabia gets much of its drinking water from the sea, and removing the salt (desalination) uses a lot of energy · a timer or a short song can help · Other side: long showers are relaxing\n\n**Rule D – Our school should plant a tree for every student.** · Facts: trees give shade and can make nearby areas cooler · trees take in carbon dioxide · native trees such as the ghaf and the sidr need little water once they grow · the Saudi Green Initiative aims to plant billions of trees in Saudi Arabia · Other side: young trees need care and water","word_count":244,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U2-LS5-1","genre":"fact files","role":"model","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[],"annotations":["Choose **one rule**. You will write an **opinion essay** that supports it.","Use the **facts** as evidence. Answer the **other side** with a counterargument and a rebuttal.","Plan **three paragraphs**: (1) hook + thesis with a preview of three reasons; (2) three reasons with evidence + counterargument and rebuttal; (3) conclusion with a call to action."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS5-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the rule cards and the model. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ بطاقات القواعد والنموذج واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Why do children take in more pollution for their size than adults?","answer":"They breathe faster than adults.","explanation":"Rule A.","explanation_ar":"لأنهم يتنفسون أسرع.","options":["They breathe faster than adults.","They are taller than adults.","They sit inside cars.","They drink more water."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why can't many recycling machines sort plastic straws?","answer":"Straws are light and small.","explanation":"Rule B.","explanation_ar":"لأنها خفيفة وصغيرة.","options":["Straws are made of paper.","Straws are too heavy.","Straws are always wet.","Straws are light and small."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"About how much water can an ordinary shower use every minute?","answer":"about 10 liters","explanation":"Rule C.","explanation_ar":"نحو 10 لترات.","options":["about 1 liter","about 100 liters","about 10 liters","about half a liter"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which native trees need little water once they grow?","answer":"the ghaf and the sidr","explanation":"Rule D.","explanation_ar":"الغاف والسدر.","options":["the oak and the pine","the palm and the apple","the bamboo and the maple","the ghaf and the sidr"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What is the 'other side' for Rule C?","answer":"Long showers are relaxing.","explanation":"Other side.","explanation_ar":"الاستحمام الطويل مريح.","options":["Showers use 10 liters a minute.","Long showers are relaxing.","Desalination uses energy.","A timer can help."],"dok":1,"skill":"counterargument","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"In the model, how does the writer answer the 'cars get hot' argument?","answer":"Drivers could park in the shade, open the windows or start the engine a minute early.","explanation":"Rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"حلول عملية.","options":["Drivers could park in the shade, open the windows or start the engine a minute early.","Cars never get hot.","Drivers should buy new cars.","Children should walk home."],"dok":2,"skill":"rebuttal","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS5-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Plan your essay. Choose.","instructions_ar":"خطّط لمقالك: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the BEST thesis for Rule B?","answer":"I believe our canteen should stop using plastic straws and cups, because they pollute beaches, are hard to recycle and have good alternatives.","explanation":"Thesis + preview.","explanation_ar":"أفضل أطروحة.","options":["Straws are long.","I drink juice every day.","The canteen is near the gym.","I believe our canteen should stop using plastic straws and cups, because they pollute beaches, are hard to recycle and have good alternatives."],"dok":2,"skill":"thesis","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which fact is the MOST relevant evidence for *Short showers save energy*?","answer":"Desalination, which makes much of Saudi Arabia's drinking water, uses a lot of energy.","explanation":"Relevant evidence.","explanation_ar":"الأكثر صلة.","options":["Desalination, which makes much of Saudi Arabia's drinking water, uses a lot of energy.","A short song can help.","Showers are relaxing.","Many homes have two bathrooms."],"dok":2,"skill":"reasons and evidence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which counterargument fits an essay supporting Rule D?","answer":"Some people say that young trees need too much care and water.","explanation":"Counterargument.","explanation_ar":"رأي معارض.","options":["Trees give shade.","Some people say that young trees need too much care and water.","Trees take in carbon dioxide.","The ghaf is a native tree."],"dok":2,"skill":"counterargument","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which rebuttal to *young trees need care* is the strongest?","answer":"However, each class could adopt a tree and water it with water saved from the school's AC units.","explanation":"Rebuttal.","explanation_ar":"رد عملي.","options":["However, I love trees.","However, each class could adopt a tree and water it with water saved from the school's AC units.","However, trees are green.","However, care is boring."],"dok":3,"skill":"rebuttal","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the canteen rule is correct?","answer":"The canteen should offer paper straws.","explanation":"should + base verb.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The canteen should offer paper straws.","The canteen should offers paper straws.","The canteen should to offer paper straws.","The canteen should offering paper straws."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is the strongest CALL TO ACTION for an essay about short showers?","answer":"Let's set a five-minute timer in every bathroom starting tonight!","explanation":"Call to action.","explanation_ar":"أقوى دعوة.","options":["Showers are important.","That is the end of my essay.","Let's set a five-minute timer in every bathroom starting tonight!","Water is wet."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS5-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Plan your essay with the Opinion Essay Planner.","instructions_ar":"خطط لمقالك بمخطط مقال الرأي.","items":[{"q":"Hook and thesis with a preview.","answer":"Hook: Our canteen gives out hundreds of plastic straws every week. Thesis: The canteen should stop using plastic straws and cups, because they pollute beaches, are hard to recycle and have good alternatives.","explanation":"P1.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة 1.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Three reasons with evidence.","answer":"1. pollute – often found in beach clean-ups; 2. hard to recycle – too light and small for machines; 3. alternatives – paper or steel straws, reusable cups","explanation":"Reasons.","explanation_ar":"الأسباب.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Counterargument and rebuttal.","answer":"Some people say that reusable cups must be washed. However, students could bring their own cups and wash them at home.","explanation":"Other side.","explanation_ar":"الطرف الآخر.","dok":3,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Conclusion and call to action.","answer":"In conclusion, saying no to plastic straws and cups would keep our beaches clean and teach good habits. Let's ask the canteen to switch by next month!","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS5-A4","type":"writing","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write, revise, edit and present your essay.","instructions_ar":"اكتب مقالك وحسّنه ودققه وقدّمه.","items":[{"q":"Write Paragraph 1: hook and thesis with a preview.","answer":"Our canteen gives out hundreds of plastic straws and cups every week, and most of them are used for only a few minutes. I believe that our canteen should stop using them, because they pollute beaches, are hard to recycle and have good alternatives.","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الأولى.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 2: three reasons with evidence, a counterargument and a rebuttal.","answer":"First, straws and cups are often among the items found in beach clean-ups. Second, plastic straws are so light and small that many recycling machines cannot sort them. Finally, there are good alternatives, such as paper straws and reusable cups. Some people say that reusable cups must be washed. However, students could bring their own cups and wash them at home.","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الثانية.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 3 of your straw-and-cup essay: the conclusion.","answer":"In conclusion, saying no to plastic straws and cups would protect our beaches and teach us better habits. Let's ask our canteen to make the change by next month!","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الثالثة.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one sentence you improved during revision (before → after).","answer":"Before: Straws are bad. After: Plastic straws are so light and small that many recycling machines cannot sort them.","explanation":"Revision.","explanation_ar":"تحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production","_w":{"checklist":[],"min":null,"max":null}}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS5-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Opinion Essay Planner","instructions":"Organizer: Choose one rule. Plan your thesis, three reasons with evidence, the other side and your conclusion.","items":[{"q":"Hook","answer":"Dozens of cars wait at our gate with engines running."},{"q":"Thesis with a preview of three reasons","answer":"Drivers should switch off their engines while waiting – protect health, reduce emissions, save fuel."},{"q":"Reason 1 + evidence","answer":"Health – children breathe faster, so they take in more polluted air for their size."},{"q":"Reasons 2 and 3 + evidence","answer":"Emissions – a running engine that isn't moving still burns fuel and releases gases; save money on fuel."},{"q":"Counterargument + rebuttal","answer":"Cars get hot without AC → park in the shade, open windows, start the engine a minute before."},{"q":"Conclusion + call to action","answer":"Cleaner air, healthier planet, fuller tanks – Let's put a friendly sign at our gate!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U2-LS5-WR","type":"writing","instructions":"Writing: Choose one Environmental Rule Card. Write a three-paragraph opinion essay (150–200 words) that supports the rule. Paragraph 1: a hook and a thesis that previews three reasons. Paragraph 2: three reasons with evidence from the card, a counterargument and a rebuttal. Paragraph 3: a conclusion that restates your thesis and ends with a call to action. Choose one Environmental Rule Card. Write a three-paragraph opinion essay (150–200 words) that supports the rule. Paragraph 1: a hook and a thesis that previews three reasons. Paragraph 2: three reasons with evidence from the card, a counterargument and a rebuttal. Paragraph 3: a conclusion that restates your thesis and ends with a call to action.","instructions_ar":"اختر بطاقة قاعدة بيئية، واكتب مقال رأي من ثلاث فقرات (150–200 كلمة) يدعم القاعدة. الفقرة الأولى: جملة جذب وأطروحة تمهّد لثلاثة أسباب. الثانية: ثلاثة أسباب بأدلة من البطاقة ورأي معارض ورد عليه. الثالثة: خاتمة تعيد صياغة الأطروحة وتنتهي بدعوة إلى العمل.","prompt":"Choose one Environmental Rule Card. Write a three-paragraph opinion essay (150–200 words) that supports the rule. Paragraph 1: a hook and a thesis that previews three reasons. Paragraph 2: three reasons with evidence from the card, a counterargument and a rebuttal. Paragraph 3: a conclusion that restates your thesis and ends with a call to action.","checklist":["My hook makes the reader care about the problem.","My thesis names the rule and previews three reasons.","My reasons are in the same order as my preview.","Each reason has relevant evidence from the card or my life.","I wrote a fair counterargument (Some people say that…).","My rebuttal (However, …) gives a fact or a practical solution.","My conclusion restates my thesis in new words and ends with a call to action.","My essay has three paragraphs and 150–200 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"rubric_id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","min_words":150,"max_words":220,"organizer":"Opinion Essay Planner (hook – thesis – reasons with evidence – counterargument and rebuttal – conclusion)","items":[{"q":"Choose one Environmental Rule Card. Write a three-paragraph opinion essay (150–200 words) that supports the rule. Paragraph 1: a hook and a thesis that previews three reasons. Paragraph 2: three reasons with evidence from the card, a counterargument and a rebuttal. Paragraph 3: a conclusion that restates your thesis and ends with a call to action.","answer":"Every afternoon, dozens of cars wait at our school gate with their engines running, filling the air with exhaust. I believe that drivers should switch off their engines while they wait, because it would protect our health, reduce emissions and save fuel. (new paragraph) First, it would protect students' health. Children breathe faster than adults, so they take in more polluted air for their size. Second, an engine that is running but not moving still burns fuel and releases harmful gases, so switching it off would cut emissions right where we stand. Finally, families would save money on fuel. Some people say that cars get too hot without the air conditioning on. However, drivers could park in the shade, open the windows or start the engine a minute before their children arrive. (new paragraph) In conclusion, a 'switch it off' rule would give us cleaner air, a healthier planet and fuller fuel tanks. Let's put a friendly sign at our gate and ask every driver to switch off and help!","_w":{"checklist":["My hook makes the reader care about the problem.","My thesis names the rule and previews three reasons.","My reasons are in the same order as my preview.","Each reason has relevant evidence from the card or my life.","I wrote a fair counterargument (Some people say that…).","My rebuttal (However, …) gives a fact or a practical solution.","My conclusion restates my thesis in new words and ends with a call to action.","My essay has three paragraphs and 150–200 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"min":150,"max":220}}]}],"game":{"name":"Green Council Debate","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Council simulator: the child picks a rule, chooses the strongest thesis, evidence and rebuttal from options; council members' faces change from doubtful to convinced. Three convinced members = rule adopted.","Each team presents its rule in one minute: thesis, two reasons with evidence and a call to action.","Another team reads a challenge card (a counterargument).","The presenting team has 30 seconds to give a rebuttal.","The council (the rest of the class) votes with green or red cards.","Rule adopted = 3 points; best rebuttal of the round = 2 bonus points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What does your environmental-rule essay try to do?","answer":"persuade readers to support a rule","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"إقناع القراء بدعم قاعدة.","options":["tell a story about a tree","describe a canteen","give a recipe","persuade readers to support a rule"],"dok":1,"skill":"genre"},{"q":"What is *exhaust*?","answer":"the gas that comes out of a car's engine","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"العادم.","options":["the gas that comes out of a car's engine","a kind of fuel","a seat belt","a road sign"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Desalination uses a lot of energy.* *Desalination* means ___.","answer":"removing salt from seawater to make fresh water","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"تحلية المياه.","options":["adding salt to food","cleaning beaches","removing salt from seawater to make fresh water","planting trees"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which part of your essay previews your three reasons?","answer":"the thesis statement","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"الأطروحة.","options":["the counterargument","the call to action","the thesis statement","the title only"],"dok":1,"skill":"thesis"},{"q":"Which sentence about the tree rule is correct?","answer":"Every student should plant one tree.","explanation":"should + base verb.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Every student should plants one tree.","Every student should to plant one tree.","Every students should plant one tree.","Every student should plant one tree."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which evidence is RELEVANT to the claim *Trees make our playground cooler*?","answer":"Trees give shade and can make nearby areas cooler.","explanation":"Relevant.","explanation_ar":"ذو صلة.","options":["The sidr tree has a nice name.","Trees give shade and can make nearby areas cooler.","The canteen sells cold juice.","Showers use 10 liters a minute."],"dok":2,"skill":"reasons and evidence"},{"q":"Which phrase introduces your rebuttal?","answer":"However,","explanation":"Signal.","explanation_ar":"However.","options":["First,","In conclusion,","However,","I believe that"],"dok":2,"skill":"rebuttal"},{"q":"Why should you choose the rule with the strongest evidence?","answer":"Strong evidence makes your essay more convincing.","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"لأن الدليل القوي أكثر إقناعًا.","options":["Strong evidence makes your essay more convincing.","Weak evidence is more interesting.","The teacher only likes one rule.","Evidence is not needed in opinion essays."],"dok":2,"skill":"evaluating evidence"},{"q":"A partner's rebuttal says *However, people who disagree are silly.* What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Answer politely with a fact or a practical solution.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"رد مهذب بحقيقة أو حل.","options":["Make it louder with capital letters.","Answer politely with a fact or a practical solution.","Delete the counterargument instead.","Add more people who are silly."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising"},{"q":"Which conclusion for Rule A is the STRONGEST?","answer":"In conclusion, switching off engines would give us cleaner air, save fuel and protect our health. Let's start a 'switch it off' week at our gate!","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"أقوى خاتمة.","options":["Cars are fast.","In conclusion, that's all.","Some people say cars get hot.","In conclusion, switching off engines would give us cleaner air, save fuel and protect our health. Let's start a 'switch it off' week at our gate!"],"dok":3,"skill":"conclusions"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'engine': a car engine under an open hood"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'exhaust': gray gas from a car's back pipe"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'fuel': a car at a petrol pump"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'desalination': a large plant beside the sea"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'native': a green ghaf tree in the desert"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'canteen': students buying food at a school counter"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'council': students around a table raising hands"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-01","prompt":"A class Green Council meeting."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-02","prompt":"Four environmental rule cards."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U2-LS5-03","prompt":"A leaf-shaped opinion essay planner."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V01","script":"engine. Switch off the engine while you wait."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V02","script":"exhaust. Car exhaust pollutes the air."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V03","script":"fuel. Waiting with the engine on wastes fuel."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V04","script":"desalination. Desalination uses a lot of energy."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V05","script":"native. The ghaf is a native tree of Arabia."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V06","script":"canteen. The canteen sells juice and sandwiches."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-V07","script":"council. Our class Green Council voted for the rule."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-T1","script":"**Rule A – Drivers should switch off their engines while waiting at the school gate.** · Facts: an engine that is running but not moving still burns fuel and releases exhaust gases · children breathe faster than adults, so they take in more air – and more pollution – for their size · some cities around the world fine drivers who leave engines running while parked · Other side: in summer, cars get hot without the air conditioning **Rule B – Our school canteen should stop using plastic straws and cups.** · Facts: straws and cups are often among the items found in beach clean-ups · plastic straws are light and small, so many recycling machines cannot sort them · alternatives: paper straws, steel straws, reusable cups · Other side: reusable cups must be washed **Rule C – Families should take showers of five minutes or less.** · Facts: an ordinary shower can use about 10 liters of water every minute · Saudi Arabia gets much of its drinking water from the sea, and removing the salt (desalination) uses a lot of energy · a timer or a short song can help · Other side: long showers are relaxing **Rule D – Our school should plant a tree for every student.** · Facts: trees give shade and can make nearby areas cooler · trees take in carbon dioxide · native trees such as the ghaf and the sidr need little water once they grow · the Saudi Green Initiative aims to plant billions of trees in Saudi Arabia · Other side: young trees need care and water"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U2-LS5-VID","script":"One small rule can make a big difference. Which rule will YOU fight for? Read the facts – and the other side – before you choose. Every afternoon, dozens of cars wait at our school gate with their engines running… Hook and thesis. Three reasons with evidence. Some people say… However… In conclusion… Let's…! Present your essay at the Green Council – and maybe your rule will become real!"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"Riyadh has a hot, dry ___, with very little rain all year.","answer":"climate","explanation":"Climate.","explanation_ar":"مناخ.","options":["carbon","landfill","council","climate"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Power stations that burn oil send ___ into the air.","answer":"emissions","explanation":"Emissions.","explanation_ar":"انبعاثات.","options":["alternatives","habits","emissions","panels"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Trees take in ___ dioxide from the air.","answer":"carbon","explanation":"Carbon.","explanation_ar":"الكربون.","options":["carbon","solar","global","desalination"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Plastic in the ocean is a ___ problem; it affects every sea in the world.","answer":"global","explanation":"Global.","explanation_ar":"عالمية.","options":["native","global","single-use","convenient"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Instead of throwing old newspapers away, we ___ them so they can become new paper.","answer":"recycle","explanation":"Recycle.","explanation_ar":"نعيد تدويرها.","options":["endanger","swallow","ban","recycle"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which word means *not able to be replaced naturally*?","answer":"nonrenewable","explanation":"non- = not.","explanation_ar":"غير متجدد.","options":["nonrenewable","renewable","reusable","enrenewable"],"dok":2,"skill":"prefixes","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*The new rule will **enable** families to sell extra solar electricity.* *Enable* means ___.","answer":"make it possible for","explanation":"en- + able.","explanation_ar":"يمكّن.","options":["stop","make it harder for","make it possible for","punish"],"dok":2,"skill":"prefixes","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*Walking to school is a healthy **alternative** to going by car.* *Alternative* means ___.","answer":"another choice","explanation":"Context clue.","explanation_ar":"بديل.","options":["a long road","another choice","a problem","a car part"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which phrase usually introduces a counterargument in an opinion essay?","answer":"Some people say that","explanation":"Signal.","explanation_ar":"Some people say that.","options":["Some people say that","In conclusion,","First,","Let's"],"dok":1,"skill":"counterargument","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which transition introduces a rebuttal that disagrees with the other side?","answer":"However,","explanation":"Transition.","explanation_ar":"However.","options":["Second,","For example,","Finally,","However,"],"dok":1,"skill":"transitions","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which is the BEST thesis for an essay about bicycle lanes in our city?","answer":"I believe our city should build more bicycle lanes, because they would reduce traffic, cut emissions and help people stay healthy.","explanation":"Thesis + preview.","explanation_ar":"أفضل أطروحة.","options":["Bicycles have two wheels.","I have a red bicycle.","Bicycle lanes are a topic.","I believe our city should build more bicycle lanes, because they would reduce traffic, cut emissions and help people stay healthy."],"dok":2,"skill":"thesis","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which family water rule is written correctly?","answer":"Families should fix dripping taps quickly.","explanation":"should + base verb.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Families should fixes dripping taps quickly.","Families should to fix dripping taps quickly.","Families should fix dripping taps quickly.","Families should fixing dripping taps quickly."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which source is MOST reliable for the claim *Desalination uses a lot of energy*?","answer":"a report from a national water and energy agency","explanation":"Reliable source.","explanation_ar":"تقرير جهة وطنية.","options":["a comment from an unknown person online","a report from a national water and energy agency","an advertisement for bottled water","a cartoon about the sea"],"dok":2,"skill":"reliable sources","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which conclusion is STRONGEST for an essay saying schools should collect AC water for their gardens?","answer":"In conclusion, collecting AC water would save water, green our gardens and teach good habits. Let's put a bucket under every unit this week!","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"أقوى خاتمة.","options":["Air conditioners are cold.","That is my essay about water.","In conclusion, collecting AC water would save water, green our gardens and teach good habits. Let's put a bucket under every unit this week!","Some people say buckets are heavy."],"dok":3,"skill":"conclusions","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"}],"unit_writing":{"task":"Write a three-paragraph opinion essay (150–200 words) answering the question: *Should our school collect water from its air conditioners to water the school garden?* Paragraph 1: a hook and a thesis that previews three reasons. Paragraph 2: three reasons with evidence or examples, a counterargument and a rebuttal. Paragraph 3: a conclusion with a call to action.","model":"Every summer day, the air conditioners at our school drip water onto the ground, while our school garden slowly dries up. I believe that our school should collect this water for the garden, because it would save water, help our plants grow and teach students good habits. (new paragraph) First, it would save water. In a country where much of our water comes from expensive desalination, we should not waste a single drop. Second, our plants would grow better, just like Nawaf's lemon tree in the story. Finally, students would learn that small ideas can protect our resources. Some people say that buckets would be messy and need too much work. However, the school could connect simple hoses from the pipes straight to the garden, so no one would need to carry anything. (new paragraph) In conclusion, collecting air-conditioner water would save water, green our garden and build good habits. Let's ask our principal to start with one hose this week!","marks":20}});
